window.TIMELINE = {"pace":{"wpm":170,"settle":3},"states":[{"id":"cover","label":"Opening","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"Networks of communicating machine agents","duration":35,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Six machines are about to work on one question. Each has seen part of the evidence, and none has seen all of it. Whether they reach the right answer depends on how capable each one is, and also on what they are allowed to say to one another, who can hear whom, and how their answers are combined at the end. This talk is about that second set of conditions.","more":["It follows the situation from a single machine to a working network: what communication adds, what it changes on the way, how such systems fail, and what the research does and does not yet establish about them.","The stage carries a label at its top right saying what kind of evidence it shows. An illustrative rule is a small model whose outcomes follow from rules the talk states, and it shows what is possible under those rules. A measured agent system reports what implemented language-model agents did in a study. Human-group studies, engineering reports, simulations and untested hypotheses are labeled as such. The label changes within a page when the argument moves from a rule to a measurement."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"systems-of-agents","label":"Systems of agents","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"AI systems can divide work among components whose outputs become one another's inputs","duration":112,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A request to an AI system can be handled by several components in turn. One component searches for documents, another reads them and extracts what bears on the question, a third drafts an answer, and a fourth reviews the draft before anything is shown to a person or carried out. Each component receives as input what an earlier component produced.","more":["Two familiar applications show the pattern. A research assistant that answers a question by searching the web, reading what it finds and writing a report with citations divides its work along these lines. So does a coding assistant that plans a change, edits files, runs the tests and revises the change after a failure. Some of the components are separate models, some are one model given different instructions, and some are ordinary programs. In every case the result is assembled from a sequence of exchanges.","The arrangement separates two kinds of behavior. A model's behavior is what it produces from a given input. The behavior of a system in which models interact also depends on which component sees which input, what each one passes on, and how the final output is assembled from the parts. A model that answers well on its own can therefore sit inside a system that answers badly, and a system can do better than any of its models would alone."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":29.2,"text":"These questions are older than language models. Research on collective intelligence asks when a group outperforms its members, and research on distributed knowledge asks how a decision can draw on information that no single member holds. Studies of coordination, social influence and network structure ask how the pattern of contacts among members changes what each one learns and what the group concludes. Distributed computing specifies what a system can still guarantee when messages are delayed, lost or forged. Each line of work supplies hypotheses about systems of communicating machine agents.","more":["The hypotheses transfer as questions, not as findings. Regularities in human groups, such as the movement of people's estimates toward the estimates of others they are shown (Lorenz et al., 2011), arise from human judgment and need not hold for language models. Whether a machine agent defers to its neighbors, repeats them or resists them can be measured directly in implemented systems. For that reason the talk labels evidence from human groups separately from evidence about machine agents, even where the two point the same way.","Many mathematical models of opinion dynamics and contagion represent communication through a numerical estimate, a discrete choice, or a change in an agent's state. These representations make it possible to study how local interactions produce collective patterns without modeling the full content of a message. Other traditions, including distributed artificial intelligence, have long represented communication through structured messages with explicit meanings and purposes (Finin et al., 1994).","Contemporary language-model agents introduce a different degree of flexibility. They can exchange extended natural-language messages containing observations, explanations, uncertain evidence, instructions, and interpretations of earlier messages. A receiving agent must interpret that content and decide how to use it. The same channel can therefore carry information that supports a conclusion, changes the terms of a question, or directs an action. Understanding the resulting system requires examining both its network structure and the content and interpretation of its messages."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":71.4,"text":"Four research problems follow from these features. Communication can improve a system's performance or merely add work, and separating the two requires a comparison that holds the work fixed. Exchange can also make the agents' judgments dependent on one another or carry an error further than any single agent would carry it. Network structure constrains which agents can exchange information, while routing, timing and message content help determine what actually reaches the point where a decision is made. Because the system's behavior arises from these exchanges, evaluating it requires evidence about the exchanges as well as about the final output.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["lorenz2011social","finin1994kqml"]},{"id":"why-study-networks","label":"Why networks","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"The behavior of a system of models depends on how its parts are connected","duration":77,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Consider a model connected to several other components. A second model can take on part of the work, retrieval supplies information, memory preserves material from earlier steps, and tools provide ways to act outside the model's immediate response. Each connection makes a particular kind of interaction possible. What the system actually does depends on which interactions occur and what information they carry.","more":["A system can fail because relevant information never reaches the component that needs it, because several agents repeat the same mistaken source, or because a correct answer is transformed before it reaches the final decision. Identifying those failures requires examining the communication process as well as the models that take part in it."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":27.4,"text":"Consider two agents that hold different pieces of information. Each can use what it knows, but neither can reconstruct what the other has observed. Connecting them creates an opportunity to combine their information. Whether that opportunity changes their decisions depends on what they communicate, whether the message arrives, and how the receiving agent interprets it.","more":["The same dependence appears in more complicated forms. Information can pass through several intermediaries, converge on a central agent, or reach a receiver along more than one path. Those arrangements decide what a receiver can know and whether several apparently separate messages are independent. A centrally placed agent may integrate evidence well or become a bottleneck, and repeated exchange may correct a mistake or make separate judgments less independent. Network science supplies ways to describe the connections, and experiments can establish what changes when the connections or the communication rules are manipulated."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":52.3,"text":"Studying these systems therefore means examining collective behavior under specified communication conditions. The same models can be arranged in different ways, given different information, and asked to exchange different kinds of messages. Comparing the resulting behavior shows which features of an interacting system contribute to its performance and its failures.","more":["The stage gives the smallest example, worked through in Act II. The same six agents, holding the same clues and joined by the same channels, reach opposite answers depending only on whether their messages carry evidence or answers. The outcome follows from a stated rule. Only the content permitted in messages changes, so the illustration shows a communication rule altering a collective result while the agents and their connections stay the same."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"one-agent","label":"One agent","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"An agent is a model inside a loop","duration":83,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Start with one call to a language model. Text goes in and text comes back. In this bare form the call keeps nothing afterward, and nothing it writes changes anything outside its reply. Many model products add memory, tools or other kinds of input, and this scene is about where those additions live.","more":["The distinction that matters here is between a model call and the decision process built around it. Whether a particular product remembers a conversation or can search the web depends on software surrounding the model, and different products draw that line in different places. For the purposes of this talk the model is the part that reads a prompt and produces text, and everything that decides what the model sees and what happens to its output is the program around it."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":24.2,"text":"An agent is a program that keeps calling the model and acts on what it returns. The program supplies standing instructions, a memory of what has happened, and a set of tools the model may ask to use. When the model's reply names a tool, the program runs it. Here the model asks for a search, the search runs, and the result comes back as something new for the model to read.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":52.6,"text":"The cycle repeats: read, decide, act, record. It continues until the model says the work is done, a rule in the program stops it, or a budget runs out (Schluntz & Zhang, 2024). The model supplies the decisions and the text. What the agent can see, what it remembers, and what it is allowed to touch belong to the program around it (Sumers et al., 2024).","more":["This division matters whenever agents are compared. A system that finds the right answer after several searches cannot be understood from its final reply alone. Its behavior depends on what the program exposed at each step and which actions it permitted, so two agents built on the same model can differ widely, and a study that changes the program has changed part of the treatment even if the model is fixed."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["s2024buildingeffectiveagents","sumers2024coala"]},{"id":"who-decides","label":"Who chooses the next step","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"The same model can sit inside three different systems","duration":68,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The model in all three arrangements is identical. In the first, it is called once and its answer is the result. In the second, a developer has written a fixed sequence of calls: draft, check, revise. The model fills in each step, but the code decides what happens next.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":20.3,"text":"In the third, the model itself chooses the next step. It can search, run code or write, in whatever order it judges useful, and it decides when to stop. Many current engineering guides call the second arrangement a workflow and reserve the word agent for the third (Schluntz & Zhang, 2024).","more":["That convention is useful for comparing applications built on language models. It is not the only usage. Research on multi-agent systems has long called autonomous, rule-governed programs agents even when their decisions follow fixed rules, and this talk uses the word in that broader sense. What the convention does capture is that arrangements differ in where control over the next step sits."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":43.8,"text":"The useful question about any system is therefore which decisions it hands to the model. A stronger model in the first arrangement still produces a single answer. A weaker model in the third chooses its own steps, possibly badly.","more":["Control is rarely all in one place. Software can fix the overall plan while letting the model choose search terms, or let the model propose actions while a separate rule decides which proposals run. Before a difference between two systems is credited to agency, it helps to list which decisions code made, which a model made, and which a person made.","Machine agents are not all language models, and the form of an agent decides what its messages can be. In rule-based systems a designer fixes both the message format and its meaning, as in the bidding messages of the contract net (Smith, 1980). Agents trained with reinforcement learning can learn their own signals, which can serve the training task without carrying meanings people can read off (Lazaridou & Baroni, 2020). Robots exchange sensor readings and state whose meaning is fixed by physics and by the designer. Language-model agents exchange natural language, whose format is open and whose meaning the receiver has to interpret. This talk concentrates on language-model agents and draws on the other forms where they established an idea first."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["s2024buildingeffectiveagents","smith1980contract","lazaridou2020survey"]},{"id":"permission","label":"Permission","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"Autonomy is granted one action at a time","duration":60,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Handing over decisions is not all or nothing. This agent may search and read on its own, and it may draft a reply. When it tries to send that reply outside the system, the program stops it and waits for a person.","more":["The stop is a rule in the program, applied to one kind of action. The agent's other powers are unchanged, and the person sees a specific proposed action, the draft and its recipient, which can be approved, changed or refused."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":20.3,"text":"Research on automation found it useful to separate four kinds of function a machine can take over: gathering information, analyzing it, choosing an action, and carrying the action out. A system can be fully automatic in the first two and entirely manual in the last (Parasuraman et al., 2000). An agent that only gathers and summarizes can mislead a person who then acts. An agent that sends, books or edits acts directly. Where a person belongs in the loop depends on which of these the system is allowed to do.","more":["The four functions also show why approval alone is a limited safeguard. A machine that selects which evidence a person sees has already shaped the decision before anyone is asked to approve it. The approver may depend on a summary the system prepared, have little time to inspect its sources, and face the same errors the system has already passed along. Approval protects a decision to the extent that the approver receives enough to judge the action and keeps the ability to refuse or change it. Act IV returns to this point with a gate placed before an irreversible action."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["parasuraman2000levels"]},{"id":"second-agent","label":"A second agent","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"Communication matters when the information is divided","duration":85,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Ask two agents whether a delivery missed its deadline. Agent 1 holds the delivery record, which says the goods arrived on May 14. Agent 2 holds the contract, which sets the deadline at May 10. Each record is accurate, and neither agent can answer: an arrival date says nothing about lateness without the deadline, and a deadline says nothing without the arrival date.","more":["This is what is meant by distributed knowledge. Everything needed for the answer exists in the system, but no single place where an answer can be formed holds all of it. Many real arrangements of agents produce this condition by design. One agent is given access to a database, another to a set of documents, a third to a tool or a person, and the task requires combining what they can each see."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":27.7,"text":"A channel opens between them, and a message carries the deadline across. Agent 1 now holds both dates, compares them, and concludes that the delivery was four days late. Neither agent became more capable. The message moved one fact to the place where the judgment was being formed.","more":["The example separates two things that are easy to run together when a group of agents outperforms one agent. One is the capacity to reason, which the channel leaves unchanged. The other is the set of facts available at the point of decision, which the channel changes. A gain that comes from the second source should disappear if the needed information is given to a single agent directly, and that substitution is one way to test which source produced it."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":50.2,"text":"Once agent 1 relies on the message, the message decides its answer. Had agent 2 misread the contract and sent May 16, agent 1 would apply the same comparison to its accurate record and the inaccurate deadline, and conclude that the delivery was on time. The failure would come from information supplied through the channel, not from any change in how agent 1 decides.","more":["The benefit and the risk of communication arise from the same dependence. Agent 1 cannot check the deadline itself, because the contract is exactly what it lacks. Whatever it concludes inherits the accuracy of the sender, and nothing in the conclusion shows that a second source was involved. Much of the rest of this talk concerns that inheritance: how messages carry it, how it compounds across many agents, and what records make it visible.","An experimenter can control this situation directly by deciding which agent holds which records. That makes three questions answerable: whether the missing information reached the agent that needed it, whether that agent used it, and whether the final answer changed because of the exchange.","Research on communicating software agents predates language models by decades. Shared blackboards on which specialist programs posted partial results (Erman et al., 1980), the contract net for announcing and awarding tasks (Smith, 1980), and agent communication languages with typed acts such as request and inform (Finin et al., 1994; Foundation for Intelligent Physical Agents, 2002) each appear later in the talk, at the point where their idea is used."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["erman1980hearsay","smith1980contract","finin1994kqml","fipa2002acl"]},{"id":"six-agents","label":"The six-clue case","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"Six agents, two explanations, unequal evidence","duration":71,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Now take six agents trying to decide which of two explanations, A or B, better fits an event. Each has examined one piece of the record. Four have found small things that point toward B. Two have found something much stronger that points toward A.","more":["The sizes on the stage carry this difference: a small ring is a weak clue and a large ring a strong one, and color shows which explanation the clue favors. This is the only place in the talk where size carries meaning. In the rule used here, a weak clue counts one point and a strong clue three."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":21.4,"text":"If we simply ask each agent for its answer and count, B wins four to two. If we instead bring all six pieces of evidence together and weigh them, A wins, because the two strong clues outweigh the four weak ones.","more":["The two procedures answer different questions. Counting answers asks how many members lean each way, and treats every answer as an equally informative signal. Pooling evidence asks which explanation the observations support, and needs a judgment about how much each observation says. In this example the weights are part of the rule. In a real system the right weights may be unknown, and several agents may have drawn on the same source, in which case their agreement says less than its count suggests.","The arrangement has a name in research on groups. Stasser and Titus called it a hidden profile: information is distributed so that each member's own evidence favors the weaker option, and only the pooled evidence reveals the stronger one (Stasser & Titus, 1985). Their groups of people often failed to find it, because discussion dwelt on what members already shared, and later work has found the pattern across many tasks (Lu et al., 2012). The design is useful here because it separates two ways a network can combine what its members know."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":43.9,"text":"The agents and their information are the same in both cases. What differs is whether the system counts answers or combines what supports them. This small case returns throughout the talk, always with the same six agents in the same places and the same clues. Its rules are stated, and its outcomes follow from those rules.","kind":"toy"}],"sources":["stasser1985pooling","lu2012twentyfive"]},{"id":"what-a-line-means","label":"What a line means","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"A line between two agents has to say what it means","duration":62,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Diagrams of agent systems draw lines between agents. A line can mean very different things. It can mean that the two are permitted to message each other, whether or not they ever do.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":14.6,"text":"It can mean that messages actually passed between them in this run, three of them here. It can also mean something with no message at all: one agent reads a file the other wrote, or both read and write the same shared memory without ever addressing each other.","more":["Each of these answers a different question. A permission says what could happen, and a designer sets it. Traffic says what did happen, and it can differ from one run to the next. A file one agent writes and another reads creates a dependency in time: the reader's work rests on the writer's, though neither addressed the other. Shared memory makes influence run both ways and leaves no record of who read what unless one is kept."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":37.1,"text":"These are different facts about a system, and a measure built on lines means nothing until the line is defined. Network researchers have long insisted that the meaning of a tie be fixed before any measure built on it is interpreted (Butts, 2009). The same holds for machine agents.","more":["The consequences are concrete. A system can have a dense graph of permissions and a sparse graph of actual messages. An agent can look isolated in the message graph while receiving most of its information through a shared database. A count of connections or a measure of brokerage computed on one kind of line can be accurate for that kind and misleading as a description of how information moved. The next act builds a network drawing from a record of messages and shows that even that drawing loses something: the order in which the messages were sent."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["butts2009revisiting"]},{"id":"the-message","label":"Inside a message","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"A message has parts, and the parts decide what a receiver can do","duration":64,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Look closely at one message as it leaves the coordinating agent. It names a sender and a receiver. It states what kind of act it is, here a request. It carries content: the question to research. It may carry context, such as the sources already found, and it says what kind of reply is expected.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":22.4,"text":"Older agent systems made these parts explicit, in formats that fixed a slot for each and let a receiver check them mechanically (Finin et al., 1994; Foundation for Intelligent Physical Agents, 2002). A message between language-model agents is often just text, in which the same parts may be present, implied or missing.","more":["The older formats drew on speech-act theory, which treats an utterance as an act with a type, such as asking, informing or promising, as well as a content. Fixing the type in a slot meant a receiver did not have to infer from the wording whether it had been asked to do something or told something."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":46.3,"text":"What a receiver can do depends on which parts arrive. Without the sources, it cannot check the claim. Without the reply format, it cannot return something the sender can use.","more":["The missing parts matter most at boundaries of authority. A sender may ask a receiver to do work, give it evidence to weigh, or pass along text copied from outside the system, and all three can arrive as similar sentences. Recording the sender, the source, the kind of act and the expected reply does not make a system safe, but it makes the relation checkable. Without those fields, a receiver can mistake an observation for a directive or a copied claim for a verified one."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["finin1994kqml","fipa2002acl"]},{"id":"message-kinds","label":"Kinds of message","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"The same words can be evidence, advice or an order","duration":60,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The same content can travel as three different kinds of message. Sent as evidence, it is something the receiver may weigh against what it already knows. Sent as a suggestion, it is a proposal the receiver may accept or decline.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":17.1,"text":"Sent as an instruction, it is something the receiver is expected to carry out. The words can be identical. What changes is what the receiver is authorized to do with them, and therefore how much damage a mistaken or malicious message can cause.","more":["The kind of message should decide what the receiving program is allowed to do next. An instruction from the system's owner may legitimately start a tool. Evidence may change what the receiver believes, and it should not by itself cause an action. When the two are handled alike, information from outside the system acquires control over it. That is a question of how authority is represented and checked as messages pass between processes, in addition to a question of language understanding."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":37.8,"text":"A well-designed system keeps those kinds apart. A document retrieved from the web is evidence, even if it contains a sentence phrased as a command. Treating it as an instruction is how an outside party can steer an agent, a failure the talk returns to in its fourth act.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"shared-board","label":"A shared workspace","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"Agents can coordinate through a shared workspace without addressing one another","duration":61,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Two agents write their findings to a shared document. A third agent reads the document and builds on what it finds. No message passed between any two of them, yet the third agent's work now depends on the other two.","more":["Coordination here runs through a shared artifact. For network analysis this means the relations worth modeling may pass through documents, queues, databases or a shared environment, and a dependency can exist between agents that never exchange an addressed message. A full account of such a system can need two networks: one of agents and messages, and one of the artifacts they write and read."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":19.6,"text":"This pattern is older than language models. Speech-understanding systems of the 1970s organized many specialist programs around a shared blackboard, on which each posted partial hypotheses and from which each took the results it could use (Erman et al., 1980). Those systems attached a level and a credibility rating to every posted hypothesis.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":41.3,"text":"A shared document written by language-model agents usually carries neither unless its designers add them. A reader of the board then cannot tell a checked finding from a guess, or one source from three.","more":["A shared workspace also brings problems that direct messages avoid or hide. Two writers can overwrite each other. A reader can act on a version that has since changed. Entries can lose their authors. Each of these is a question about time and provenance, and Acts III and IV return to both."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["erman1980hearsay"]},{"id":"fair-comparison","label":"A fair comparison","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"A group can look better simply because it did more work","duration":86,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A group of agents often looks more capable than one agent because it has been allowed to do more work. Five agents that each answer three times use fifteen calls to the model. A single model asked once uses one. When the group wins, the comparison cannot say whether talking helped or whether fifteen tries simply beat one.","more":["The extra resources are a competing explanation. Repeated independent answers, one model revising its own answer, and agents exchanging messages can all improve on a single response, and they do it through different uses of the same resource. An experiment that credits communication has to rule out the simpler routes to the same gain."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":26,"text":"A fair test gives the alternatives the same budget. The same fifteen calls can be spent on one agent that keeps working with tools, on fifteen independent answers that are then counted, on a fixed sequence of drafts and checks, or on agents that exchange messages.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":45.2,"text":"Equal budgets make the comparison more informative without identifying what communication contributed. A network that exchanges messages may also divide the task differently, assign specialized roles, or combine its answers by a different procedure, and any of those differences could affect the result. Independent answers counted together establish what repeated attempts achieve without communication. To credit communication itself, the agents, their information, the task, the budget and the final decision rule have to be held fixed while only the permitted exchange changes. A difference can then be attributed to communication under the conditions tested.","more":["A count of model calls is a coarse budget. One call can read a few hundred words or many thousands, and a call can trigger tool use whose cost is separate. Two designs with the same number of calls can therefore differ several-fold in the text processed and in time. A fair comparison states which budget was held fixed, whether calls, tokens, tool use or time, and reports the others."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"sampling-baseline","label":"Independent answers","act":"Act I · One agent becomes a network","heading":"Independent answers counted together are a strong baseline","duration":61,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The baseline that communication most needs to beat involves no communication at all. Ask the same model the same question many times, let the answers vary, and take the most common one. This simple procedure improves accuracy on many reasoning tasks (Wang et al., 2023), and in one large study accuracy kept rising as more answers were added, by amounts that depended on how hard the task was (Li et al., 2024).","more":["Counting independent answers helps for a statistical reason. When a model's errors on a question vary from one sample to the next, the most common answer is more often right than any single sample, much as averaging independent measurements reduces noise. The gain depends on independence. If the errors are shared, because every sample makes the same mistake, more samples add nothing."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":30.9,"text":"When researchers have given debating agents and independent voters comparable budgets, much of the advantage credited to debate has turned out to come from the voting (Choi et al., 2025). The comparison establishes what repeated independent sampling achieves without communication. An exchange among agents needs to improve on that baseline, at a comparable budget, before the difference can be credited to the exchange.","more":["Exchange can add what sampling cannot, when agents hold different information or when one agent's message lets another do something it could not do alone. It can also take away what sampling relies on. Agents that read one another's answers no longer err independently, so their agreement carries less information than the same number of independent answers. Act II measures both effects."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["wang2023selfconsistency","li2024moreagents","choi2025debatevote"]},{"id":"from-record","label":"From record to drawing","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"A network drawing is made from a record, and the record has an order","duration":99,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A real system leaves a record before it leaves a picture. Watch a briefing task run. A person asks for a two-page briefing. An orchestrator splits the request between two researchers, who search, read, and write notes into a shared draft. A writer drafts from the notes, a reviewer checks the draft, and the reviewer reports back to the orchestrator. Each message adds one line to the log: who sent it, to whom, at which step, and what kind of message it was.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":32.3,"text":"Now draw the network from that log. Every pair that exchanged a message gets a line, and the lines thicken with repeated traffic. This is where most network drawings of agent systems come from, and it already carries a decision about what a line records.","more":["The log is the primary observation. Each line is an event with a time, a sender, a receiver and a kind, and everything said later about the system's structure is computed from these events. A drawing is one summary of them, and the choice of summary is a choice about what to keep."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":54.7,"text":"The drawing has lost the order. It shows a route from the reviewer through the orchestrator to the writer, and a reader would conclude that the reviewer's objection could have reached the writer. In the log it could not. The orchestrator's only message to the writer came at step 6, and the reviewer reported at step 10. Information can follow a sequence of messages only if each message is sent after the previous one arrived. A drawing that ignores order shows paths that never existed, so it overstates how far information could have moved (Holme & Saramäki, 2012).","more":["Network science calls such a sequence a time-respecting path, and the set of agents an agent can influence along such paths its temporal reach. Temporal reach is usually smaller than the reach a static drawing suggests, and it is not symmetric: the reviewer could have influenced the orchestrator here, but nothing the reviewer said could have reached the writer before the writer drafted. In this run the objection had no route to the agent who could act on it.","The same record supports several drawings. A drawing of permitted channels shows what the system allows, a drawing of observed traffic shows what it did, and a time-ordered replay shows what could have reached whom. Position measures computed on one of them describe that drawing only. Two agents can differ in position in different ways: one can have the most channels, another can sit on the only route between two groups. Measures of centrality formalize these different ideas, and they agree only in special graphs (Freeman, 1978). Everything that passes between two groups joined by one channel must cross that channel, which makes it both the route for integration and a single point of failure, a theme the robustness scenes return to."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["holme2012temporal","freeman1978centrality"]},{"id":"one-message","label":"Three networks","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Who can talk to whom decides how far a message must travel","duration":71,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Who can talk to whom determines how many steps information needs to reach the rest of a system. Watch one message start at the same agent in three arrangements. The agents and the message stay the same. Only the connections change.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":17.5,"text":"In a chain, the message passes from neighbor to neighbor and needs several relays to reach the far end. In a star, every path runs through one central agent: the message reaches the hub at once and the hub passes it on. In a fully connected network, every agent hears it directly.","more":["The count that differs is the number of relays, which network science measures as path length. In a chain of six the far end is five relays away. In a star every agent is at most two relays from every other. With full connectivity every agent is one relay from every other."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":41.3,"text":"Each arrangement has a cost. The chain needs many relays, and every relay is a chance for the message to change. The star needs few, but it depends on one agent and makes that agent read everything. Full connectivity needs the fewest relays, but the number of messages grows quickly with the number of agents, and every agent must read all of them.","more":["Fewer relays do not guarantee a faster or better result. Elapsed time also depends on how long each agent takes to read and respond, and an agent that must process many messages can become the slowest step in the system. The work of integrating many contents can outweigh the relays saved. The next scene separates that reading load from what the messages contain."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"reading-load","label":"Reading load","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"How much an agent must read is not how much it knows","duration":58,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Every message is text that its receiver must read before it can act. The number of messages an agent receives in a round is called its fan-in. In the fully connected network each of six agents receives five messages every round. At the hub of a star, the central agent receives five while each of the others receives one.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":23.8,"text":"An agent can receive five messages that carry the same clue five times, or one message that carries five different clues. Fan-in measures load, not knowledge. For a language model, load has a direct cost: more to read, more chances to overlook the part that matters.","more":["The distinction is between traffic and information. Five copies of one clue are five messages and one piece of evidence, and an agent that counts them as five has been misled by the count. One message carrying five independent clues is light traffic and a great deal of evidence. Whether the copies are recognized as copies depends on whether the messages say where their content came from, the label that the evidence-exchange scene relies on and that Act III examines."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":45.6,"text":"That distinction becomes important shortly, because one experiment found that hearing from more agents at once did not always help.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"evidence-exchange","label":"Evidence exchange","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"When agents share their evidence, the strong clues reach everyone","duration":82,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Return to the six agents, now connected so that every agent can message every other. In one round, each agent sends its clue to all five others. The message carries the clue itself, with the direction it points and its weight, and a label that tells a receiver which clue it is.","more":["The label matters more than it seems. A receiver that can identify each clue can take the union of what it holds and what arrives, so a clue that reaches it twice is still counted once. Act III shows what happens when that label is missing and the same clue arrives by two routes."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":23.9,"text":"Follow the top agent. It began with one weak clue for B and now holds all six clues: four weak clues for B and two strong clues for A. Weighed together they favor A, so the top agent moves to A. Every other agent receives the same six clues and applies the same rule, and the whole group arrives at A, including the four agents that began at B.","more":["The disagreement at the start came entirely from unequal information. Every agent applies the same rule to whatever it holds, so once all of them hold the same clues, nothing remains to disagree about. The two agents that held strong clues keep A for the same reason the others abandon B: the pooled evidence points there.","This is the outcome a hidden profile is designed to test. In the experiments with people that introduced the design, the decisive information is spread so that each member's private evidence points to the inferior option, and the group can find the better one only by pooling what its members hold (Stasser & Titus, 1985). Here pooling is guaranteed by the rule. The later scenes ask what happens when it is not."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":56.2,"text":"The group reaches the pooled answer because three conditions hold at once: every agent can hear every other, the messages carry the evidence itself, and each agent weighs what it holds instead of counting heads. Change any one of them and the outcome can change. The next scene changes only the second.","more":["Each condition is stipulated in this example, and each becomes a measurable property in a system of language-model agents. Messages can fail to arrive or arrive late. A receiver can fail to recognize that two reports describe the same observation. A model can give a quoted observation more or less weight than it deserves, and nothing guarantees that its weights resemble the ones written into this example. The example shows what pooled evidence can do when those conditions hold."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["stasser1985pooling"]},{"id":"conclusion-exchange","label":"Conclusion exchange","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"When agents share only their answers, the majority wins","duration":68,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Keep the agents, their private evidence and the network exactly as they were, and change only what the messages carry. Each agent now sends its current answer, A or B, and nothing else.","more":["An answer compresses everything behind it into a single choice. That saves reading, and it removes three things a receiver would need to weigh the answer: what evidence produced it, how strong that evidence is, and whether several answers rest on the same source. Four agents that each hold a weak clue for B now look exactly like four agents with strong grounds for B."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":17.1,"text":"Follow the agent at lower right, which holds a strong clue for A. Under the rule used here, it counts its own answer with the five it hears: two for A and four for B, so it moves to B. Its strong clue has not changed, and no other agent has ever seen it.","more":["The rule is a simple one, chosen so that its consequences are easy to follow: each agent adopts the majority of the answers it can see, its own included, and keeps its answer on a tie. A receiver that gave its own evidence more weight, or discounted answers it could not check, would behave differently. Whether language-model agents resemble this rule, or any other, is a question about measured behavior, and the next scene takes it up."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":41.7,"text":"The other strong agent gives way for the same reason, and the whole group ends at B, although the evidence it holds favors A. The evidence and the network are as they were in the previous scene. Under this rule, the type of message decided the answer.","more":["Counting answers is a reasonable way to aggregate when each answer rests on comparable and independent grounds. In this case the grounds are unequal, and the count measures how many agents lean toward B, which is a different quantity from the weight of evidence for B. The same group, holding the same clues, reaches opposite conclusions depending on whether its messages carry clues or answers."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"measured-agents","label":"What measured agents did","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Language-model agents sharing answers became unstable","duration":95,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The illustrative rule predicts one kind of failure: the group settles on the answer favored by the larger number of first impressions, and it does so in every run, because the rule has no randomness. Language-model agents can fail differently.","more":["This is the point where the talk moves from a stated rule to a measurement, and the difference between them holds for every later pair of this kind. An illustrative rule shows what can happen under assumptions chosen to be transparent. It cannot say whether implemented agents behave that way, how often, or under which models and prompts. A measurement can, for the agents, tasks and conditions actually tested, and it says nothing beyond them without further evidence. The label at the top right of the stage changes with the passage that follows."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":19.6,"text":"In a controlled experiment, networks of agents were given the same kind of conflict, a larger group with weaker evidence for the wrong answer and a smaller group with stronger evidence for the right one. The agents exchanged either their evidence or only their conclusions (Gladstone, 2026).","more":["Each agent received a private reading and reported a probability for each answer, and the network's answer was read by pooling those probabilities with a rule fixed before the experiment. What the agents sent was assigned by the experimenter, so the comparison between evidence and conclusions is a controlled contrast. The paper describes conclusion sharing as a limiting case of messages that carry a judgment without its grounds, and does not claim that deployed systems exchange bare verdicts."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":41.7,"text":"With five agents who all heard one another, sharing evidence largely preserved the group's initial accuracy. Sharing only conclusions made the group less accurate in two of the three model families tested. The loss came mainly from instability: different runs of the same network ended in quite different places, rather than consistently converging on the wrong majority.","more":["The reported values are collective accuracies with confidence intervals. With evidence sharing, accuracy was 0.90, 0.96 and 0.97 in the three model families. With conclusion sharing it fell to 0.64 and 0.62 in two of them, while the third stayed highly accurate under both conditions. Within trials, agents became less accurate while their beliefs became more dispersed, which is the opposite of the uniform drift to the majority that the illustrative rule produces (Gladstone, 2026).","The study also tested the influence of conclusions directly. Holding every agent's private evidence fixed, it planted one confident early conclusion. A correct plant raised the collective's terminal probability for the true answer to 0.99 and 0.85 in two families, and an incorrect plant lowered it to 0.36 and 0.34. In one family, allowing the agents extended private reasoning under conclusion sharing raised accuracy from 0.62 to 1.00, a result the authors treat as identifying one possible moderator."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":69.8,"text":"The experiment also varied how many peers each agent heard. At the highest level tested, sharing evidence became less accurate than sharing conclusions, so more evidence from more agents did not always help. The study measured outcomes, not the agents' internal reasoning, so why that reversal occurs remains open.","more":["At that level, eleven agents each heard all ten others. Evidence sharing reached 0.38 against 0.58 for conclusion sharing when a weak majority opposed a better-informed minority, and 0.68 against 0.96 when every private signal favored the truth. Because the decline occurred even when all the evidence pointed the right way, deference to a misleading majority cannot explain it. A heavier load of evidence to read and combine is one candidate explanation, and the study did not test it, which connects this result to the reading-load scene. Across the network manipulations, accuracy fell and capitulation by the better-informed minority rose as the number of incoming messages grew."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["gladstone2026accuracy"]},{"id":"chain-relay","label":"A chain","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"In a chain the group agrees before anyone holds all the evidence","duration":90,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Return to the six agents and their clues, and connect them as a chain, so that each agent can talk only to the one or two beside it. They exchange evidence, as before. In every round each agent passes along all the clues it has gathered so far, labeled so that none is counted twice.","more":["Two quantities change from round to round, and the stage keeps them apart. The letter on each agent is its current judgment. How much of the evidence it holds is a separate matter, and in a chain the two can diverge for several rounds."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":24.9,"text":"After one round, five of the six agents judge A. Each of them now has a strong clue within reach, its own or a neighbor's. Only the agent at one end, which has heard from a single neighbor with a weak clue, still judges B. From the second round on, every agent judges A.","kind":"toy"},{"at":47,"text":"Yet the clues are still traveling. The two agents nearest the middle hold all six only after the third round, and the agents at the two ends only after the fifth, the length of the chain. The group was right long before anyone was fully informed. That happened because the strong clues sat near the middle, within a relay or two of most agents. Placed at the two ends instead, they would have taken longer to meet, and some agents would have changed their answer on the way.","more":["The general point concerns path length and position together. The time for a clue to reach every agent is set by the longest distance it must travel, which in a chain of six is five relays. The time for the group to reach the right answer is set by something else: how far the decisive clues are from the agents whose judgments count. A chain can be slow to inform and quick to decide, or the reverse, depending on where the evidence starts.","Being right before being informed has a cost that the toy does not show. An agent that judges A on partial evidence has no way to know whether the clues still on their way will confirm or reverse it. If the system stops early, or reads its answer from an agent that has not yet heard everything, the answer reflects the arrangement of the chain as much as the evidence."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"star-hub","label":"A star","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"In a star the hub knows everything after one round, and the others catch up a round later","duration":79,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Rearrange the same agents so that every channel runs through one of them, the hub. Here the hub is one of the four agents whose own clue is weak. The agents and their clues have not moved. Only the channels have changed.","kind":"toy"},{"at":17.8,"text":"In the first round every agent sends its clue to the hub, and the hub sends its own clue out. The hub now holds all six clues and judges A. Every other agent holds two, its own and the hub's. The three whose clues are weak now hold two weak clues and still judge B. The group stands at three against three.","more":["The hub reaches the pooled answer in one round because it is one relay from everyone. The others depend on it entirely: what they learn about the rest of the network is whatever the hub chooses to forward, and in this round the hub has forwarded only its own weak clue."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":45.2,"text":"In the second round the hub forwards everything it holds, and every agent judges A. Whether the star is fast therefore depends on where its answer is read. Read at the hub, it is right after one round, with 10 messages per round instead of the 30 of the fully connected network. Read by majority, it is tied after one round. The hub also carries the whole load, and every other agent depends on it.","more":["The star separates two properties that are easy to conflate. The hub is central in the network sense: every path passes through it, and it reads every message. That position gives it the first complete view of the evidence. It does not make the hub's own clue more reliable, and it does not by itself give the hub authority over the group's answer. Whether the hub's view becomes the group's answer depends on the readout, which the next scenes make explicit, and on whether the hub forwards what it holds faithfully, which Act IV tests by making it fail."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"bridge","label":"A bridge between groups","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"One bridge sets the pace between two groups, and carries only what it is given","duration":111,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Now divide the six into two groups of three. Within each group every agent can reach the other two. Between the groups there is a single channel, a bridge. The lower group holds both strong clues, and the upper group holds only weak ones. After one round of evidence exchange, each group has pooled its own clues. The lower group is unanimous for A and the upper group for B. The agent at the upper end of the bridge has received a strong clue across it, but the four clues it now holds cancel, so it keeps B.","kind":"toy"},{"at":37.6,"text":"In the second round everything the lower group has gathered crosses the bridge. The bridge agent now holds all six clues and judges A. Its two neighbors, one step further away, hold four clues that cancel and keep B. Only in the third round does the evidence reach them, and the whole group agrees. Every clue that passes between the groups crosses the same channel, so the bridge sets the pace.","more":["Dense groups joined by sparse ties are a familiar pattern in social networks, and the tie between the groups has a particular role. Within a group, members quickly come to hold the same information, so further messages among them add little. Across the bridge, each message can carry something the receiving group has not seen. The bridge is the network's only route for that new information and also its single point of failure, which Act IV examines by cutting it."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":68.1,"text":"Keep the groups and the bridge, and change only the messages to conclusions. After one round the lower group is unanimous for A, because its one agent with a weak clue hears A from both neighbors. The bridge then carries a single A into a group of three Bs, where it is outvoted on arrival, and a single B the other way. From then on nothing changes, and the groups stay three against three. Whether a bridge joins two groups depends on whether it carries what the other group knows or only what it thinks.","more":["Under conclusion exchange, each group becomes internally unanimous before anything crosses, and a single answer arriving across the bridge cannot outweigh a local consensus. The connection exists and carries messages every round, and yet it never changes a judgment. A network can therefore be connected in the graph-theoretic sense while remaining divided in what its members believe."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"where-read","label":"Where the answer is read","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"The same frozen network gives three answers, depending on where it is read","duration":95,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Freeze the two groups where the conclusion exchange left them: three agents at A, three at B, and no further change however long they continue. The network has not yet produced an answer. Someone has to take one from it, and the rule for doing so is called the readout.","kind":"toy"},{"at":20.6,"text":"Take a majority of all six, and the network returns no decision. Read one designated agent, the lower end of the bridge, and it answers A, the answer the pooled evidence supports. Read the agent at the top of the upper group instead, and it answers B. Nothing inside the network changed between these three results. Only the readout moved.","more":["The readout is part of the system being evaluated, in the same way that the choice of a measure is part of a measurement. A vote, a designated reporter, a final synthesizing agent and a human reading the transcript are different instruments applied to the same internal state, and they can disagree. Many published systems fix the readout implicitly, by passing everything to an orchestrator or by taking the output of the last agent in a pipeline."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":47.3,"text":"A comparison of two networks is therefore a comparison under a readout. Set the divided network beside the fully connected one, which ended at B. Read at the lower bridge agent, the divided network finds the right answer where the fully connected one does not. Read by majority, it only avoids the wrong answer. Read at the top agent, the two do not differ. Every published comparison takes its answer from somewhere: a vote, an orchestrator, the apex of a hierarchy. If every agent's final output is logged, several readouts can be computed from the same runs, and a report can show whether its result survives the change.","more":["This is a cheap robustness check because it needs no new runs. It also guards against a specific error of interpretation: crediting a difference to the shape of the network when it comes from which agent's output was taken as the answer. The measured study later in this act read networks without a built-in integrating agent from one agent chosen at random, and its authors report that result as belonging to the network and its readout together."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"task-change","label":"Changing the task","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Move only the clues, and two architectures trade places","duration":93,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Now hold two architectures fixed and change the task. On the left is a hierarchy. Agents report upward, two middle agents pass what they have to one agent at the top, and nothing flows back down. On the right is the star from before. Both are read by majority.","kind":"toy"},{"at":20.3,"text":"With the clues where they have been all along, the hierarchy's majority is right after one round, because each middle agent has a strong clue among the agents reporting to it. The star's majority is tied after one round and right after two. On this task, the hierarchy looks like the better design.","kind":"toy"},{"at":42,"text":"Now move the two strong clues, so that they sit with the top agent and with one middle agent. The weights and the pooled answer are unchanged. The star's majority is now right after a single round. The hierarchy's majority is never right, because four of its six agents hold only weak clues and nothing comes back down to them.","more":["The change is to the task, in the sense that matters for a network: which agent holds which piece, and therefore which pieces must meet where. The answer the evidence supports is identical in both versions. What changed is the pattern of dependence between agents, the same property that separates work that can be split into independent parts from work in which each step needs the result of another."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":68.7,"text":"Neither architecture is better in general. Which one looks better depends on how the information is allocated, how the answer is read and when the system is stopped. When the answer depends on combining particular pieces held by particular agents, the arrangement decides when and where those pieces meet.","more":["The ranking of two architectures on one task is therefore a fact about the pair of architecture and task. A study that tests several architectures on one kind of task can rank them for that kind. It cannot rank them in general, and a ranking that reverses when the task changes is evidence that the task's structure, as much as the architecture's, produced the result."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"search","label":"Search","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Fast copying can end the search on the lower hill","duration":106,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A second kind of collective problem is search. Each agent holds a candidate solution, drawn here as a position in a search space with a low hill and a high one, and can score any solution it holds. In each round an agent copies a neighbor's solution if it scores strictly better than its own, and otherwise takes one step uphill if it can. Copying spreads what works. Stepping explores.","kind":"toy"},{"at":27.7,"text":"In the fully connected network every agent sees the best solution at the start, which sits on the low hill, and copies it in the first round. By the third round all six sit at the low peak. Nothing is left to explore, and the search is over.","kind":"toy"},{"at":47.6,"text":"On a ring, the agent that began on the slope of the high hill sees only two neighbors, and both hold worse solutions. It keeps climbing, and once its solution scores better than theirs, they copy it. By the eighth round all six sit at the high peak. The fully connected network was ahead only for the first two rounds.","kind":"toy"},{"at":71.8,"text":"This is the trade-off that March (1991) described in organizations, between refining what is already known and searching for something better, and that Lazer and Friedman (2007) simulated in networks: efficient networks converge quickly, while slower ones keep more variety and can find better solutions later. The toy shows a possible mechanism, not how often it operates. What protected the high hill here was that its one explorer saw nothing better-looking until it had climbed.","more":["The outcome follows from two local rules, copy the better neighbor and otherwise climb, and nobody chose the result. Connectivity changes how quickly the copying rule erases variety, and variety is what the climbing rule needs in order to find the higher hill. The same rules on a search space with a single hill would give full connectivity an unqualified advantage. The next scene shows that when people searched a similar problem, the advantage went the other way, and why."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["march1991exploration","lazer2007network"]},{"id":"human-groups","label":"Experiments with people","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Experiments with people made the channel pattern a variable, and their results are hypotheses for agents","duration":117,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Comparing networks is older than machine agents. Around 1950, Bavelas and his colleagues began asking how a pattern of communication channels affects a working group (Bavelas, 1950). In the laboratory design that followed, each member of a small group held part of the information, the group had to assemble it and reach a decision, and the experimenter fixed who could send messages to whom (Guetzkow & Simon, 1955). That is the structure of the six-clue case, and the manipulation carries over directly: an orchestration program fixes the channels as firmly as the experimenter did.","more":["The design is what transfers. A study of agents can fix the channels, distribute the information, and measure how the group's answer depends on both, exactly as the laboratory studies did. The findings are a different matter. They describe people: how people attend to messages, how they weigh their own information against what they hear, and how they respond to status and to the views of others. Language-model agents may share some of these tendencies, none of them, or tendencies of their own, and only experiments on agents can say which. In this talk a result from a study of people is a hypothesis about agents until an agent study tests it. This paragraph states that limit once for every study of people the talk cites."],"kind":"human"},{"at":38.7,"text":"The outcomes carry over less well. The search toy reproduces the pattern that Lazer and Friedman simulated, in which slower networks found the better solution. When Mason and Watts (2012) had people search a similar problem space online in groups of 16, efficient networks did better, and networked groups beat people searching alone. A later simulation traced the disagreement to how individuals copy: copying the best neighbor favored slow networks, and copying the common choice favored fast ones (Barkoczi & Galesic, 2016). In an agent system the copying rule is set by prompts and protocol, so it has to be measured, not assumed.","kind":"human"},{"at":78,"text":"Estimation experiments point the same way. Becker et al. (2017) had people estimate quantities, see the average of their neighbors' estimates and revise. In decentralized networks, where everyone had the same number of neighbors, the group's estimates became more accurate. In centralized ones, the central person's initial error could spread to everyone. The improvement rested on a regularity in people: the more accurate revised less. Whether language-model agents show anything like it is an open question. These studies supply a method and some predictions, not evidence about agents.","more":["The estimation result shows how a network effect can rest on an individual-level regularity. Decentralized exchange improved accuracy because the people whose estimates were closer to the truth held to them more firmly, so averaging with neighbors pulled the others toward them. Remove that correlation between accuracy and confidence, and the same network would only make estimates more similar without making them better. For agents, the corresponding question is whether a model's willingness to revise tracks how good its own answer is, which is measurable and not yet settled."],"kind":"human"}],"sources":["bavelas1950communication","guetzkow1955impact","barkoczi2016social","mason2012collaborative","becker2017network"]},{"id":"agent-studies","label":"Measured agent networks","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Measured architecture effects in agent networks come bound to a task and a readout","duration":115,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Studies of language-model agents have now made similar comparisons. Kim et al. (2025), a preprint, compared single agents with four multi-agent architectures under matched compute on six agentic benchmarks. The largest gain over a single agent, 80.8 percent, came from a centralized architecture on a financial-reasoning task that splits into separable parts. The largest loss, 70.0 percent, came from independent agents on a sequential planning task. The two extremes differ in architecture and in task at once, so the contrast between them cannot say which of the two mattered.","more":["The direction of the comparison depended strongly on the task. Coordination helped on work that could be divided into parts and hurt on tasks in which each decision depended on the one before, the same distinction the task-change scene drew with six agents. The preprint also reports that errors grew more when independent agents' outputs were combined than when a central agent coordinated them, which it offers as a candidate explanation. The values are from version 3 of the preprint (arXiv:2512.08296v3, revised April 8, 2026), which reports 260 configurations. The percentages are relative changes against a single agent on the same task, not percentage points, and they describe the architectures and tasks tested."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":36.9,"text":"Gladstone and Brashears (2026) compared six architectures of Claude-family agents on puzzles in which each agent held one clue and no clue sufficed alone, the structure of the six-clue case. A tiered hierarchy solved all 10 sampled trials. The fully connected network solved 1 of 10. In networks without a built-in integrating agent, the answer was read from one agent chosen at random, so that result belongs to full connectivity read in that way. On open-ended synthesis tasks, differences among the architectures were not statistically resolved.","more":["The authors suggest premature consensus as one candidate explanation for the fully connected network's failure: early candidate answers can circulate before the constraints needed to rule them out have been combined. Their data do not measure that process, and they say that transcripts of the exchanges would be needed to distinguish it from other explanations. On the simple tasks, centralized architectures also used fewer rounds and roughly half as many tokens as decentralized ones (Gladstone & Brashears, 2026)."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":72.8,"text":"Li et al. (2024) varied only the channels within one debate protocol. Sparse graphs, in which each agent read a few neighbors instead of everyone, matched or exceeded the accuracy of full connection on their tasks while substantially reducing the text each agent had to read. None of these studies holds the agents, the models and the protocol fixed while varying the task across kinds. Until one does, the evidence supports a narrow conclusion: architecture effects for language-model agents have been observed, they differ by task, and each is defined by the readout under which it was measured.","more":["The sparse-debate result connects to the reading-load scene and to the measured agents earlier in this act. Fewer incoming messages meant less to read, and accuracy did not suffer on the tasks tested. Together with the decline in accuracy at the highest fan-in in the collective-accuracy study, it suggests that the number of messages an agent must integrate is a design variable in its own right, separate from how well connected the network is."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["kim2025scalingagents","gladstone2026topology","li2024sparse"]},{"id":"debate-baseline","label":"Debate","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"A debate has to beat a vote that receives the same number of answers","duration":100,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"One widely studied protocol for exchange among language models is debate. Several instances of a model answer a question, read one another's answers and reasoning, revise, and after some rounds their answers are combined. Du et al. (2024) reported that three instances debating for two rounds outperformed a single instance and a majority vote on arithmetic, grade-school math and chess-move tasks.","kind":"measured"},{"at":24.5,"text":"That comparison was not matched. The vote combined the three first answers, three calls in all. The debate went on to produce three more answers in each round, nine in all. The first act showed what happens when a vote receives as many answers as the debate: much of the advantage moves to the voting. A single agent with a strong prompt has also come close to the best discussion method on reasoning tasks (Wang et al., 2024).","more":["The matched comparison isolates what exchange adds beyond sampling. Debate does two things at once: it draws more answers from the model, and it lets each instance revise after reading the others. A vote over the same number of answers reproduces the first without the second, so any advantage that survives the matched vote can be credited to the revision."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":57.6,"text":"Discussion can also make answers worse. In debates among models from three families, Wynn et al. (2025), a workshop paper, found that accuracy could fall over the rounds as agents switched from correct answers to incorrect ones after reading their peers' reasoning, even in groups where the stronger models were the majority. On the reasoning benchmarks tested so far, debate has often failed to beat independent answers from the same model at a comparable number of calls. Those benchmarks give every agent the same question and the same information, which is not the situation of the six-clue case.","more":["That last condition matters for interpretation. When every agent already holds all the information, exchange can only correct reasoning errors or introduce new ones, and the evidence so far suggests it does both. When agents hold different information, exchange can also move evidence to where it is needed, the benefit the six-clue case was built to show. Debate benchmarks have mostly tested the first situation, so their results say little about the second."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["du2024debate","wang2024rethinking","wynn2025talk"]},{"id":"scripted-majority","label":"A scripted majority","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Shown a scripted majority, language models moved toward it","duration":104,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"To isolate one ingredient of such failures, researchers have borrowed a design from social psychology. In the 1950s, Asch had people judge the lengths of lines after confederates had unanimously given a wrong answer (Asch, 1951). The machine version places one model among scripted peers. The model answers alone first. It is then shown several peers' answers, all identical and carrying no evidence, and asked again.","more":["The peers on the stage are scripts. They do not reason, read or respond, and the experimenter writes their answers in advance. That is what makes the design informative about the model: everything it is shown is under the experimenter's control."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":28.8,"text":"The manipulation is the message. The model's own information is held fixed, and only the answers it is shown change, so a change in its answer is an effect of that exposure. Zhu et al. (2025) found that every open model they tested, of seven to nine billion parameters, moved toward the presented majority whether its first answer was right or wrong, and moved more when it was uncertain. A benchmark of such protocols found a tendency to conform in every model it evaluated, including very large models under some protocols (Weng et al., 2025).","kind":"measured"},{"at":65.3,"text":"These designs measure a response to a presented majority. They do not measure a disposition to be persuaded, or how often wrong majorities arise in free exchange. They also strip the peers' messages of evidence, which is exactly the condition under which the six-clue rule made the strong minority give way. The measured agents earlier in this act did not simply follow that rule: sharing conclusions made them unstable rather than uniformly wrong. Conformity is one candidate mechanism among several, alongside limited attention to many messages at once.","more":["The measured response is a change in the model's answer after it is shown a scripted majority, under the prompts and tasks tested. Calling it conformity borrows a word from the study of people, where it refers to a motive. The design does not establish a motive in the model, and it does not show how the same model would behave in a network whose members exchange evidence and respond to one another. It identifies sensitivity to presented answers, which is one input to how a freely communicating network behaves."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["asch1951effects","weng2025benchform","zhu2025conformity"]},{"id":"chosen-graphs","label":"Graphs chosen per task","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Some systems choose who talks to whom for each task","duration":83,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Every architecture so far was fixed before the system ran. A further family of designs treats the graph itself as something to choose. One represents an agent system as a graph and optimizes its connections along with its prompts (Zhuge et al., 2024). Another generates a communication graph for each new task (Zhang et al., 2025). A third starts dense and prunes the messages it judges redundant, to reduce what agents must read (Zhang et al., 2025). These systems were read only at the level of their abstracts, so what they do is reported here, not how well they do it.","more":["The three differ in when the choice is made. Optimizing a graph happens before deployment, over many tasks. Generating a graph happens once per task, before the agents start. Pruning happens during or between runs, using what the system has observed about which messages mattered. The last is close to the partner choice of Act IV, where each agent decides as the work proceeds and the structure forms from those decisions."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":41.1,"text":"A procedure that chooses the graph is itself an architecture one level up, with its own costs and its own readout. Its gains can be credited to the graph only if the comparison holds the agents, the model calls and the protocol fixed. In the six-clue case the graph that is needed is easy to state: the strong clues must reach the agents whose answers are read, by the round at which they are read. For most tasks that requirement is not known in advance. Deciding at run time who does what, and who must hear from whom, is the problem of coordination.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["zhuge2024gptswarm","zhang2025gdesigner","zhang2025agentprune"]},{"id":"divide-work","label":"Dividing work","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Dividing a task creates work that a single agent never had to do","duration":99,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Turn from what a network concludes to how its agents get work done. This is the briefing workflow. A person asks for a short briefing on flood-sensor coverage. An orchestrator splits the request into two sub-questions, two researchers search and write notes into a shared draft, a writer turns the notes into a briefing, and a reviewer checks it before it goes back.","more":["The same five actors appear in the record-to-drawing scene and return in Acts IV and V. Their roles are fixed by the program: who may call which tool, who may write to the draft, and who decides when the briefing is finished."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":27.7,"text":"Splitting the request created work that one agent doing everything would not have faced. Davis and Smith (1983) described distributed problem solving as four activities: decomposing the problem, distributing the pieces to solvers able to handle them, solving the pieces, and synthesizing a result. Here the orchestrator decomposes and distributes. The researchers solve, each without seeing the other's results. The writer synthesizes from whatever reached the draft.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":54.4,"text":"A fifth activity is deciding that the work is done. Here it passes through three actors: the reviewer, which can return the draft, the orchestrator, which decides to submit it, and the person, who decides to release it. Each activity can fail on its own. A decomposition can leave a question unassigned, a synthesis can use only what reached it, and a stopping decision can come too early or never. A taxonomy built from annotated traces of multi-agent language-model systems located failures in system design, in the interaction between agents and in verification, as well as inside single model calls (Cemri et al., 2025).","more":["Each division also creates an interface: a product that one actor hands to another and that has to be complete enough for the receiver to do its part. The notes must carry their sources for the reviewer to check them. The draft must say which sub-question each claim answers for the orchestrator to see whether both were covered. The next scenes take three of these interfaces in turn: how work is assigned, what a handoff carries, and what happens when a promise to do work is not kept."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["davis1983negotiation","cemri2025whymasfail"]},{"id":"contract-net","label":"The contract net","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"The contract net assigns work by announcement, bid and award","duration":101,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"In the base workflow the orchestrator assigns sub-questions by a fixed rule. An older design lets the agents negotiate. In the contract net, which Smith (1980) introduced for networks of cooperating computers, a manager announces a task, the nodes able to do it send bids, the manager awards the task to one bidder, and that contractor later reports its result. A contractor can subcontract in turn, so a hierarchy of control forms while the work proceeds instead of being fixed in advance.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":31.9,"text":"Here the orchestrator announces the second sub-question with an eligibility condition: a bidder must be able to call the search tool. The writer and the reviewer cannot, so they do not bid. Both researchers bid, reporting whether they are busy. The idle one receives the award and later returns its result. Smith argued that eligibility conditions reduce needless message traffic, a design rationale his paper states but does not measure.","more":["The four messages are distinct acts with distinct consequences. The announcement creates no obligation. A bid is an offer. The award turns the offer into a commitment for the bidder and a dependency for the manager, which now plans around a result it does not yet have. The report discharges the commitment. A system that records each act can tell, at any moment, who owes what to whom."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":62.1,"text":"The protocol carries over to language-model agents with one change in what a bid means. Smith's nodes computed their bids from their own state. A language-model agent asked whether it can do a task answers in text, and a model's description of its own competence is not a calibrated estimate of it. A manager that selects on such bids may select on self-description, a possibility no study read for this talk has measured. The bids here avoid the problem by reporting facts the program knows: whether the agent holds the tool, and whether it is busy.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["smith1980contract"]},{"id":"handoff","label":"A handoff","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"A handoff without its state can defeat the check that follows it","duration":123,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A handoff passes a task from one agent to another, with some of what the first agent knew. Hold the rest of the workflow fixed and vary only what the orchestrator's instruction to the writer carries. The researchers wrote seven notes, each linked to its document. The writer turns every note it receives into a claim, keeping the link if one came with it, and adds two claims of its own with no source. The reviewer flags every claim without a link it can open.","kind":"toy"},{"at":33,"text":"With the notes and their links, the draft has nine claims, seven of them sourced, and the reviewer flags the writer's two. Paste the notes' text without the links, and the draft again has nine claims, but the reviewer flags all nine. The check has not become stricter. It can no longer tell the seven grounded claims from the two that are not.","kind":"toy"},{"at":58.2,"text":"Now hand over only the first researcher's notes, links intact. The draft has six claims, four of them sourced, and the reviewer flags two, exactly as before. The check passes, the second sub-question is missing, and nothing in the system reports it. A missing piece produces no claim to flag, so only an actor that knows what the whole task required can catch it, here the orchestrator or the person, not the reviewer.","more":["The reviewer works correctly on what it is given. Its check is local: it can judge each claim in the draft, and it has no view of what the draft should have contained. Completeness is a property of the draft relative to the task, and it can be checked only by an actor that holds the task's full specification. That is a design choice about where knowledge of the whole is kept."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":90.5,"text":"The return to the writer opens a loop, and the loop needs a rule for stopping. Stop after the reviewer's first pass, and flagged claims go out unrepaired. Stop only when the reviewer flags nothing, and a reviewer that always finds one more problem keeps the loop running indefinitely. A round limit, a budget and a person's approval each guarantee something different. Two systems that differ only in when they stop can deliver different briefings from identical exchanges.","kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"disagreement","label":"Disagreement","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"Voting settles a disagreement among agents, not a conflict between sources","duration":82,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Suppose the researchers' notes conflict. One reports, from one document, that district 4 has had sensors since 2021. The other reports, from a different document, a sensor failure in district 4 in 2020. Both cannot be right as written.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":16.8,"text":"A vote asks each agent which it believes and takes the majority. The researchers split, so the writer and the reviewer decide the outcome, although neither has read either document. A consensus procedure has the two researchers exchange reasons until they agree, which they eventually do. Agreement, however, can come from persuasion or deference as easily as from the documents.","more":["Both procedures aggregate what the agents believe. Neither consults the evidence that produced the beliefs, so their outcome can be no better than the agents' reading of documents that, in the vote, two of the four voters never saw."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":43.4,"text":"The third procedure sends both notes, with their links, to an agent that reads the two documents. It may find that one document is wrong, that the two describe different sensors, or that the briefing should report the discrepancy. Voting and consensus aggregate positions, and in debate studies they have improved answers to questions that every agent attempts on its own (Kaesberg et al., 2025). This conflict lies between sources. The information that would settle it is in the documents, not in any count of what the agents believe.","more":["The distinction runs through the rest of the talk. A disagreement can arise because agents reason differently from the same information, in which case aggregation over their answers can help. Or it can arise because their information differs, in which case the useful step is to bring the information together. The six-clue case is a disagreement of the second kind, and conclusion exchange failed it for the same reason a vote would fail here."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["kaesberg2025voting"]},{"id":"commitment","label":"A dropped commitment","act":"Act II · Communication changes what a group can do and conclude","heading":"A commitment has to be a record that the program keeps","duration":97,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"When the second researcher accepts its sub-question, the orchestrator plans around that acceptance and later dispatches the writer on the assumption that both sets of notes will be there. Suppose the search tool fails and the researcher gives up. If it sends nothing, the writer drafts on schedule, the briefing covers the first sub-question only, and nothing in the log records that the commitment ended.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":25.9,"text":"If instead the researcher tells the orchestrator that it cannot finish, and why, the orchestrator can reassign the work, wait, or tell the person that the briefing will omit it. The two runs differ by one message, and that message decides whether the omission is a known decision or a silent gap. Classical work on cooperating agents treated commitments, and conventions for monitoring them, as the foundation of coordination (Jennings, 1993).","more":["A commitment couples two agents across time. The one that accepted the work constrains its future, and the one that relied on the acceptance has built its plan on it. Jennings's conventions specify when a commitment may be dropped and what the dropping agent owes the others, chiefly a message saying so. The second run is that convention in action."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":56.5,"text":"Those theories describe the states of idealized agents. A language model that writes \"I will do this\" holds no such state, because nothing persists between its calls unless the program records it. A commitment in such a system has to live outside the model, as a task record with an owner, a status and a deadline, and a rule for what happens on failure, such as a required report or a timeout after which the work is reassigned. That is a design inference from the classical accounts, not a result tested on language-model agents.","more":["A commitment kept in a task record remains available for later agents to inspect, but the information carried in that record can still be incomplete or misleading. The next problem concerns what happens when agents repeatedly summarize, reinterpret or combine the information on which their decisions depend."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["jennings1993commitments"]},{"id":"relay","label":"A sentence passed along","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"Four rewrites keep the number and change the claim","duration":140,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"An agent that passes a report along can do more than copy it. It can reword the report for a new reader, shorten it, or merge it with others, and each of those operations is a chance for the content to change. Follow one sentence through four agents, each asked to restate what it received. The sentence says that a preliminary study of one district found that a new routing rule may cut response times by about 10 percent, according to the transport office. Besides its number, it carries five qualifications: the study is preliminary, the effect may occur, it was seen in one district, the figure is approximate, and a named office stands behind it.","more":["Each qualification tells a reader something different about how far to trust the figure. Preliminary says the evidence is early. May says the effect is uncertain. One district bounds where it was observed. About says the number is rounded. The office names who measured it, which is what a reader would need to check it."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":46.4,"text":"The first agent rewords the sentence and keeps every qualification. The second drops the preliminary study and turns may cut into will cut. Nothing it wrote is plainly false, and the number is untouched, yet a tentative estimate has become a confident forecast. The third agent changes the tense. The rule now cut response times, so a forecast has become a reported outcome. The same agent drops the district and the word about, and the transport office becomes officials. The fourth drops the attribution altogether and adds that the rule is being rolled out citywide, which no earlier message said.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":86.7,"text":"The routing rule and the 10 percent survived every hop. Almost everything that told a reader how far to trust the figure did not. Each edit is a different kind of change: a hedge removed, a projection restated as an outcome, a scope widened, a figure made more exact, a source replaced and then lost, and a statement added without support. Several of them leave the central assertion intact and alter only its epistemic status, meaning whether the claim presents itself as a guess, a forecast or an established fact, and on whose authority. These rewrites were written for this talk to show one kind of change at a time. They are not model outputs and say nothing about how often models make such edits.","more":["The changes are of different kinds, and they call for different defenses. A lost hedge or a changed tense alters what the sentence asserts. A widened scope alters what it applies to. A lost attribution removes the means of checking it. An added statement creates content with no source at all. A system that guards against one, for instance by checking that no unsupported sentence appears, can miss the others entirely, because nothing in the fourth version is new except the citywide program."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"token-check","label":"A check that passes","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"A check for preserved words passes at every hop","duration":153,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Suppose the relay is checked automatically, by asking whether the key terms of the source still appear: the routing rule, response times and 10 percent. The check reads each version in turn. All three terms appear in the source and in every rewrite, so the check passes five times out of five. Checks of this kind are attractive because they are cheap, deterministic and easy to explain, and a pipeline that passes one can look as if it preserved what it carried.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":31.9,"text":"A second check compares what each version asserts with what the source asserted: whether the effect is still presented as possible, still confined to one district, and still attributed to the office that measured it. The source and the first rewrite pass. From the second rewrite on, the check fails, and by the fourth the sentence reports a citywide program with a measured effect, which the source never claimed. The first check was not wrong about what it measured. The words survived. The mistake would be to read their survival as evidence that the claim survived.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":68.8,"text":"Three properties of a passed-on message come apart here. Its tokens are the strings a check can match. Its meaning is what it asserts. Its justification is what supports the assertion and who stands behind it. A token check can establish only the first. The fourth sentence reads like a report of evidence, yet it carries no more of its support than a bare answer would, the same loss that sharing answers instead of evidence produced in the previous act.","more":["The gap between the two checks is a problem of construct validity. A measure can be reliable, giving the same result every time, and still measure something other than what it is used to stand for. The token check reliably measures whether strings survived. Used as a measure of whether the claim survived, it is invalid, and the invalidity is invisible from its own results, since it passes every time."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":102.6,"text":"Research has measured parts of this process. When ten language models summarized scientific texts, most stated conclusions more broadly than the originals did, even when asked for accuracy (Peters & Chin-Yee, 2025). That is a change of scope at a single step. Transmission-chain studies, in which each model retells the previous retelling, have tracked which content survives and how whole texts drift. No study found for this talk tracks, hop by hop, whether the qualifications attached to a claim survive as language-model agents relay it, so the steady accumulation in the relay is a hypothesis about agents. A study of implemented agent architectures measured part of it.","more":["Two designs supply most of the evidence about how models transform text. The first compares one output with its source. Maynez et al. (2020) had human annotators read summaries produced by several neural summarization systems, all of which predate current large language models, and found substantial amounts of content unsupported by the source in the summaries of every system. Peters and Chin-Yee (2025) compared summaries of scientific texts written by ten large language models with the original texts. For DeepSeek, ChatGPT-4o and LLaMA 3.3 70B the rate of overgeneralization ranged from 26 to 73 percent of cases, and in a direct comparison the model summaries were nearly five times as likely as summaries written by people to contain broad generalizations (odds ratio 4.85, 95 percent confidence interval 3.06 to 7.70).","The second design is the transmission chain, in which each generation retells what the previous generation produced. Acerbi and Stubbersfield (2023) ran five preregistered transmission-chain experiments with ChatGPT-3, using materials from earlier human studies, and found that the model, like people, preferentially retained content that was consistent with gender stereotypes, social, negative, threat-related or biologically counterintuitive. These studies measure which content survives and how properties of whole texts drift. They do not track whether the qualifications attached to a particular claim survive.","Repeated transmission also changes which details remain prominent. In a transmission chain each new message is produced from the previous message, not from the original source. An omission or reinterpretation at one step therefore shapes every later version. Perez et al. (2025) observed attractor-like changes in text properties such as toxicity and length across iterated language-model retellings, with the strength of the pattern varying by model and by how open-ended the instructions were. Those findings identify one way repeated transformation can change the information available to downstream agents."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["peters2025generalization","maynez2020faithfulness","acerbi2023transmission","perez2025telephone"]},{"id":"mutation-study","label":"Six architectures","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"Implemented architectures differed in depth as well as in shape","duration":111,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The same question can be asked of measured agents. Gladstone and Brashears (in press) passed seven seed messages through implemented networks of language-model agents, and every agent was told to rewrite what it received so as to capture its meaning accurately. Two of the networks show the range. In a hub with spokes, a message reaches its delivered output after two rewrites. In a linear chain of eleven agents, it is rewritten eleven times.","more":["Six architectures were built in all: the linear chain, a ring with shortcuts, the hub with spokes, a directed hierarchy, a lattice, and a bidirectional lattice in which two parallel chains exchanged feedback in a debate stage before revision. Seven seeds passed through each, giving 420 chains from seed to delivered output. Language-model coders scored the outputs on three kinds of change. Drift is movement away from the seed within one chain, measured as fidelity to the seed and completeness. Fabrication is content with no traceable support in the seed. Divergence is how far separate chains carrying the same seed end up from one another."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":31.6,"text":"The six architectures differed in more than their shape. Along the shortest route a message travels, the seed passed through between 2 and 14 rewrites, and a chain used between 11 and 28 model calls. Role prompts, sampling settings and the demand to merge two inputs also changed with the architecture, so each architecture is a bundle of these features.","more":["That bundling sets what the comparison can show. A difference between two architectures is a difference between two bundles, and the design cannot assign it to the graph, to the number of rewrites, or to any other feature that changed alongside (Gladstone & Brashears, in press). The authors therefore asked how far the differences could be separated from depth. Under the primary coder, ordinary single-input rewrites at depth 2, with the same prompt, model and temperature, showed no detected difference in fidelity or completeness across the architectures that contain them. Comparisons at matched depth found no excess of fabrication specific to any architecture that held up across coders.","The longest dependent path differs from the shortest route in some designs. In the bidirectional lattice the debate stage feeds the final output through 17 dependent rewrites, and the study reports both definitions in its Table 1."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":60.8,"text":"Under the model condition every architecture shared, all three coders detected differences among architectures in fidelity to the seed. Differences in unsupported content were less consistent across messages and coders. A difference in how far separate runs diverged was detected by the primary coder only, and neither independent coder nor an embedding measure reproduced it.","more":["Coding by more than one model family was part of the design for a specific reason. The transmitting agents and the primary coder came from the same model family, so two coders from other families recoded a frozen sample of 2,581 outputs under a protocol deposited before the recoding began. Each independent coder gave consistent scores when it scored the same outputs again. Their agreement with the primary coder was moderate for fidelity, completeness and fabrication, and essentially absent for whether factual details were retained.","That is why the divergence result is reported as the primary coder's alone. It describes how one instrument classified the outputs, and the study does not treat it as an established property of the architectures. Most of the contrasts that survived correction for multiple comparisons involved the bidirectional lattice, whose differences remain entangled with its debate stage and its greater depth."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":88.2,"text":"Within the linear chain, a post hoc check by both independent coders found fidelity falling and unsupported content rising from the first agent to the eleventh. In one measured structure, change accumulated with the number of rewrites, as the four-step relay suggested it could.","more":["The scores measure how far an output moved from its seed. They do not say which kind of change occurred, whether a condition was lost, a scope widened, or a projection restated as a result. The next scene follows one such change through a measured run. The study's own conclusion concerns method: evaluating a pipeline of agents requires attention to how many rewrites a message has undergone, and to coding by more than one model family (Gladstone & Brashears, in press)."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["gladstone2026mutation"]},{"id":"status-shift","label":"A documented case","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"In one measured run, a projection became an outcome with its figure intact","duration":107,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"One run in that study shows the relay's central change in measured agents. A seed message contained a conditional projection, that a change \"would increase spoilage losses by approximately 23 percent\". In one of the bidirectional lattice's two parallel chains, the phrase had already been strengthened to \"will lead to\" before the debate stage began. When that chain was revised in response to feedback written about the other chain, the statement became a stated outcome. It reached the delivered output with its numerical markers intact.","kind":"measured"},{"at":33,"text":"A check for preserved names and numbers would have passed this output. The authors call a strengthening of epistemic status that leaves the factual markers in place epistemic-status fabrication. One case is not a rate, and the authors do not treat it as one. A search of all 60 runs from that seed found this exact conversion in one. A person reviewing the end of a pipeline can still receive an output whose evidentiary status has changed while every figure in it is correct.","more":["The case is useful as a demonstration that the relay's change occurs in measured agents, at the step the relay predicts and with the same surviving markers. It is not evidence of how often it occurs, which requires a measure applied across many outputs. The study built one."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":68.1,"text":"To measure such shifts more broadly, the two independent coders judged, for 12 propositions in the seed messages, whether each one's status had changed in an output. They met the agreement criterion set in advance for 9 of the 12. For one proposition they disagreed sharply, one coder marking a shift in 18.5 percent of outputs and the other in 0.3 percent. Status shifts occurred for several propositions, at rates specific to the proposition and to the coder, and the instrument did not record whether the figures survived those shifts.","more":["The two estimates for that proposition describe how two instruments classified the same outputs under this protocol. They do not support choosing either as the prevalence of the underlying change. What the disagreement shows is that judging epistemic status is itself a measurement problem: two careful coders can apply the same written criterion and reach very different counts, so a rate of status drift needs validation beyond one model's judgment. For four of the twelve propositions, both coders rarely marked a shift, in no more than 2.2 percent of outputs. In the documented run, the 23-percent proposition did not meet the agreement criterion, while a related projected-cost proposition did, and both independent coders marked its status as shifted in that run's delivered output."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"counted-twice","label":"One clue, two routes","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"Without labels, a clue that arrives twice is counted twice","duration":133,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The six agents return with their clues unchanged. Score each weak clue as one point for B and each strong clue as three points for A. Pooled once, A leads by two points. Number them clockwise from the top. This time the channels form a relay that ends at agent 1, which adds up everything it hears. Agent 3 sends its strong clue to two relays, agents 2 and 6. Agents 4 and 5 send their clues to agent 6. Each relay then forwards everything it holds to agent 1. The messages carry the clues but not the names of the agents that found them.","kind":"toy"},{"at":40.1,"text":"As the messages land, agent 1 adds up what it holds: its own weak clue, two clues from agent 2, and four from agent 6. It counts seven clues where six exist, and finds A ahead by five points instead of two. Nothing in the messages tells it that two of the strong clues are one clue that arrived by two routes.","kind":"toy"},{"at":65.9,"text":"Trace the two strong clues back, and both paths lead to agent 3. This is double counting: one piece of evidence enters the sum once for each path that delivered it. Here the answer stays A, and only its strength is inflated. If a weak clue had been duplicated instead, the error would push toward B, and enough duplicates would reverse the answer. The error grows with the number of redundant paths, so channels added to make a network resilient can make it worse.","more":["Treating the points as weights of evidence makes the size of the error concrete. In the natural-log units of these weights, a total of 2 corresponds to odds of about 7 to 1 for A, and a total of 5 to about 150 to 1. One duplicated message multiplied the apparent confidence twentyfold while the evidence stayed the same."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":101.1,"text":"Nothing in agent 1's rule is faulty. Adding weights is correct when each weight is a separate observation, and the network made two of them the same observation without saying so. Agents that read one hub's summary, one retrieved document or one another's answers receive dependent information of the same kind, and agreement among them can look like more evidence than it is.","more":["The rule assumes that the weights it adds are conditionally independent: given the true answer, each observation carries information the others do not. Redundant paths break that assumption without changing any message's content. Work on animal and human groups reached a related conclusion by another route. In two simple models of binary decisions, Kao and Couzin (2014) found that when the cues available to individuals are correlated, accuracy is often maximized by a small group, because adding members adds correlated information instead of independent evidence. Agents that share a base model, a retrieved document or a hub's summary receive correlated inputs of this kind, so the result is a hypothesis for machine agents, not a finding about them."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["kao2014decision"]},{"id":"provenance-record","label":"A provenance record","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"When each clue carries its origin, the count is restored","duration":119,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Now let every clue travel with a record of where it came from: the agent that found it and the agents it passed through. Agent 1 receives the same seven clues as before. Because each one names its origin, agent 1 can see that two of them come from agent 3, keep one entry for that clue, and note both routes. Its count returns to six clues and A ahead by two, the pooled value. The network, the rule and the clues are unchanged. Only what a message carries has changed.","kind":"toy"},{"at":35.1,"text":"This is a minimal provenance record: for each item, its origin and the route by which it arrived. Names alone would have prevented the double count here. The routes do more. They show that agents 2 and 6 are not independent with respect to agent 3, which matters if a later step weighs the relays' own judgments or decides which channel to trust. Larger systems keep the same relations as a graph of messages, agents and source documents, recording which message was derived from which input by which agent.","more":["The record turns a question that was unanswerable from the messages into one that can be answered by lookup. It does not make the network smarter. It gives the receiver the information that the independence assumption needed and the messages had dropped, the same role the label played in the evidence-exchange scene of Act II."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":73,"text":"A record of this kind has to be kept by the system, not by the agents. The relay showed an attribution drift from the transport office to officials and then disappear as a sentence was rewritten. A source named only in the text of a message is exposed to the same loss, because an agent asked to rewrite a message may rewrite its citations too. The record is reliable when the surrounding program attaches it outside the rewritable text and checks it when messages merge. Even then it shows what the network did with messages. Whether two agents observed the world independently is a further fact, recorded only if someone recorded it at the origin.","kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"three-reports","label":"Agreement and its sources","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"Three agreeing reports can rest on three sources or on one","duration":92,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Agreement is persuasive because it seems to count. Three reports that reach the same conclusion look like three confirmations. Here are three small systems whose outputs are identical where a reader receives them: three reports, each concluding A. What differs lies upstream, out of the reader's view.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":19.6,"text":"In the first system, three agents each read a different source and draft a report, and their agreement reflects three sources. In the second, one agent reads one source and drafts once, and three other agents each rewrite that draft, so all three reports rest on a single upstream message. A hub that broadcasts its summary to every worker, or a memory that several agents read, creates the same dependence. In one simulated community of language-model agents, knowledge manipulated in one agent spread to others through ordinary exchange and persisted in the chat histories that agents later retrieved (Ju et al., 2026).","kind":"schematic"},{"at":58.6,"text":"In the third system, three agents draft separately, but all three read the same document. No message passed between them, yet their reports depend on one source. These are different facts. The second system's dependence lies in the network's messages, which can in principle be inspected. The third lies in what the agents observed, and no inspection of the messages alone settles it. A count of agreeing reports answers neither question. It counts messages.","more":["In the language of causal inference, the second and third systems share a common cause: one draft, or one document, upstream of all three reports. Reports derived from a common source cannot be counted as independent confirmations of that source's claim. Separate readings of one source can still carry information about how reliably its contents were read. Three agents reading the same document could agree because it is clear, because they share an interpretive bias, or because they reproduce the same error. What agreement establishes therefore depends on the question asked of it. Agreement among transformations of one source bears on the fidelity of transmission, whereas the correctness of the source's claim requires evidence independent of that source. Reports from distinct sources are not necessarily independent confirmations either, because distinct sources can share an upstream origin of their own. Telling these cases apart requires information about the producing process, either a provenance record or, as the next scene asks, whatever traces the process left in the reports themselves."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["ju2026flooding"]},{"id":"message-residue","label":"Reading structure from outputs","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"Comparing reports with one another revealed a shared draft","duration":160,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"When a network keeps no record, the only evidence about its structure is the messages that leave it. A controlled experiment asked whether those messages carry residue of the network that produced them (Gladstone, 2026). Language-model agents drafted short incident reports from the record of one constructed event, and other agents rewrote the drafts. Each set of three reports came from one of three assigned structures: three separate drafts each rewritten once, one draft rewritten three times, or a Y in which two of the three reports shared a draft. The question was how well the structure could be inferred from the three reports alone.","kind":"measured"},{"at":40.1,"text":"Every report was one rewrite of one draft, so by design a single report looks the same under every structure. Any residue had to lie in how the reports relate to one another. For the main producing model, a classifier given only summaries of each report's own features separated sets with a shared draft from separately drafted sets with an area under the curve of 0.699, where 0.5 is chance and 1 is perfect separation. Given the similarities between pairs of reports as well, it reached 0.908. The gain was smaller but positive for producers from two other model families.","more":["The reported intervals were 0.657 to 0.743 for the classifier given single reports and 0.875 to 0.937 for the one given relations between reports. The gain from relations was also positive against three more flexible models of a set (Gladstone, 2026). The design is an inverse problem posed under controlled conditions: the experimenters knew the producing structure, so they could measure how much of it the outputs revealed."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":80.9,"text":"Most of that signal was plain text reuse. In checks added after the main results, one statistic, the overlap of rare word sequences between two reports, captured most of it, and classifiers given single reports whose texts were not shared with other sets were equivalent to chance. After up to five further rewrites, the comparison of reports kept most of its margin over chance while the summary of single reports lost most of its own. The inference also made errors. Once Y sets were included, the exact structure was assigned correctly for 64.1 percent of sets.","more":["The rare-sequence overlap alone reached 0.863 on the two-way task. The checks on unshared texts and the single unfitted statistic were added after the main results, and the three-way analysis that included Y sets was exploratory, so those results carry that status."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":121.2,"text":"The inference chose among the three structures the experiment assigned. The networks were small and synthetic, the reports short and from one genre, and no agent tried to disguise a shared draft. The results say nothing about the direction of transmission, the identity of agents, missing intermediate messages or the reconstruction of arbitrary networks. Shared facts also make reports alike. When drafting agents saw overlapping parts of the record, discrimination became easier, so similarity alone cannot attribute a resemblance to a shared message rather than to shared source material, the third system of the previous scene.","kind":"measured"}],"sources":["gladstone2026residue"]},{"id":"mixing-models","label":"Mixing models","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"A mixed vote helps only when its members err in different places","duration":142,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Shared sources make agents' evidence dependent, and models trained on overlapping data may also make correlated errors. One motivation for combining different models is that models trained differently may make different mistakes. Whether a combination gains depends on two quantities that pull against each other, how often each member is right and how far their errors overlap. A majority vote of three makes the trade-off visible. The group answers a question correctly when at least two members do, so it fails exactly on the questions that two or more members miss.","kind":"toy"},{"at":35.1,"text":"Three samples of one model each answer 9 of 12 questions. Their errors coincide on the two hardest questions, so the vote gets 10. Three different models, each just as accurate, make errors that overlap on only one question, and the vote gets 11. Diversity helped because the members were equally accurate and wrong in different places.","kind":"toy"},{"at":58.2,"text":"Now join the best model with two weaker ones that each answer 6 correctly and share four errors. The vote falls to 8, below the best model alone. Agreeing with each other, the weaker members outvote the best one where it was right, and a vote cannot tell their agreement from correctness. These answer patterns were written for this talk to separate the two quantities, and the vote is computed from them.","more":["The same two quantities govern the wisdom of crowds in human judgment and ensembles in statistics: a combination improves on its members when they are individually competent and their errors are not too strongly related. Correlation of errors is the machine analogue of the shared sources earlier in this act. A vote among three samples of one model is partly a vote among three copies of the same tendencies."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":89.1,"text":"Studies of combined language models have found both outcomes. A mixture-of-agents system, in which several different models propose answers and another model merges them, was reported to lead an instruction-following benchmark (Wang et al., 2025). When samples of the single best model replaced the different proposers, the result scored higher, and the authors traced the difference to mixing lowering the average quality of the proposals (Li et al., 2025). Assigning each model to the function it performed best has been reported to improve existing multi-agent systems (Ye et al., 2025). The gap in quality between members and the overlap of their errors are candidate explanations, but no study found for this talk varies the two separately at an equal budget.","more":["The mixture-of-agents comparison was not matched for computation and was scored by model judges (Wang et al., 2025). The self-mixture scored 6.6 percent above the mixture on AlpacaEval 2.0 and 3.8 percent above it on average across benchmarks including MMLU, CRUX and MATH (Li et al., 2025). That pair of studies is the closest available to the comparison on the stage, because it replaced different proposers with samples of one model while keeping the merging procedure.","That substitution tests whether the diversity of the proposers contributes enough to offset the lower average quality of their proposals. It does not separate the overlap of errors from proposal quality, because both changed when the proposers changed. Separating them would require varying the accuracy of the members and the dependence of their errors independently."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["wang2025mixtureofagents","li2025selfmoa","ye2025xmas"]},{"id":"role-labels","label":"Roles and labels","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"A label written into a prompt did not by itself improve performance","duration":68,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Many multi-agent systems assign roles in text: a critic, a planner, an expert in some field. Whether the label does the work has been tested in a few senses. Across 162 roles and 2,410 factual questions, adding a persona to a single model's instructions did not improve accuracy compared with no persona (Zheng et al., 2024). That is a result about one model answering alone. It does not test whether different roles in a group spread attention across different parts of a problem.","more":["A role label and a role are different things. The label is text in a prompt. The role, in the sense that matters for a network, is a set of obligations and powers the program enforces: which tool the agent may call, which inputs it receives, what it must produce and who reads it. A critic that is given the sources and required to check each claim against them has a different job from a model told that it is a critic."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":34.8,"text":"A related label tells an agent where its inputs came from. In two studies of networks of language-model agents, telling the agent that combined the others' work that one input came from a more capable system produced no reliable improvement (Gladstone & Brashears, 2026; Gladstone & Brashears, in press). The authors read this narrowly. The labels gave no reliable benefit under their procedure, which does not show that agents ignore such information.","more":["Two further senses of role have been tested. Liang et al. (2024) set two debaters against each other in assigned positions with a third model as judge, an example of roles used as a design device. Baltaji et al. (2024), a workshop paper, assigned agents national personas in cross-cultural collaboration and debate and found that discussion could diversify collective decisions, but that agents also conformed under perceived peer pressure and sometimes confabulated, so their roles eroded. In the transformation study, an exploratory measure showed a small shift toward the labeled source in one configuration, and in the topology study an early advantage of declared capability on simple tasks shrank as the sample grew."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["zheng2024personas","gladstone2026topology","gladstone2026mutation","liang2024mad","baltaji2024persona"]},{"id":"hub-evidence","label":"Who sits at the center","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"With evidence exchange, the agent at the center shapes what others believe along the way","duration":113,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Keep the six agents, their clues, the update rule and the number of rounds fixed. Connect them as a star, and change only which agent sits at the center. On the left, the hub holds a weak clue for B. On the right, it holds a strong clue for A. Messages carry identified evidence, so no clue is counted twice. This is the simplest form of a question that arises whenever one agent in a network differs from the rest, the question of where it should sit.","kind":"toy"},{"at":34.7,"text":"After one round, both hubs hold all six clues and judge A, and every other agent holds two clues, its own and the hub's. What the outer agents conclude from those two differs. Beside a hub with a weak clue for B, the three outer agents with weak clues still see more support for B and stay there, while the two with strong clues stay at A, so the group is split three to three. Beside a hub with a strong clue for A, every outer agent sees A ahead and moves to A.","kind":"toy"},{"at":71.9,"text":"After a second round, every agent in both stars holds all six clues and judges A. The time at which complete information reaches everyone is a property of the star, two rounds in either case. What agents believe along the way depends on what the hub contributes. With the strong clue at the center, the network looks unanimous and correct after one round, although five of its six agents have seen only two clues. If the answer were read from an outer agent after one round, placement would decide it.","more":["The hub's position amplifies whatever it holds. In one round its clue reaches five agents, while an outer agent's clue reaches only the hub. Placing the strong clue at the center did not change what the network eventually knows. It changed the order in which the agents learned it, and with an early readout the order is the answer."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"hub-answers","label":"The hub under answer exchange","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"With answer exchange, the agent holding the decisive clue is outvoted at the center","duration":104,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Change only what the messages carry. Each agent now sends its answer and adopts the majority of its own answer and those it hears, as in the previous act. With the weak clue at the center, the hub hears three Bs and two As, keeps B, and sends B to everyone. The outer agents with weak clues already agree. The two with strong clues face a tie between their own A and the hub's B and keep A. The group stays split four to two, and the hub reads out B.","kind":"toy"},{"at":36.1,"text":"With the strong clue at the center, the hub hears four Bs and one A. Its own A is outvoted, and it switches to B after the first round. Its strong clue never leaves it in a form anyone can weigh. Only one outer agent, the other holder of a strong clue, keeps A. The placement that produced early unanimity under evidence exchange makes the best-informed agent the one most exposed to the majority.","kind":"toy"},{"at":65.2,"text":"A central position gives an agent more inputs and more influence over what others receive. Whether that helps depends on what the protocol lets it send and how it weighs what it hears. The example varies what the hub knows, not how well it reasons, so it says nothing directly about a stronger model at the hub. It suggests a hypothesis: a more capable hub would spread a better integration if it receives and weighs evidence, and would be the agent most exposed to the majority if it only counts answers.","kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"placement-measured","label":"Measured placement","act":"Act III · What messages become, and where they came from","heading":"Measured effects of placing a stronger model were directional or absent","duration":97,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The two preceding demonstrations varied which evidence sat at the center of the network. The measured study addresses a different intervention, which position a stronger model occupies. Gladstone and Brashears (2026) tested placement with language-model agents. Networks solved constraint puzzles and open-ended synthesis tasks, and in some conditions one agent used a more capable model from the same family. In a seven-agent hierarchy, putting the stronger model at the top was associated with higher quality on the synthesis tasks. More runs had been added to those conditions after an initial sweep, and in the later runs alone the estimate kept its direction but was imprecise, so the authors treat the benefit as directional.","more":["The toy and the study are related but not interchangeable. The toy changes what the hub knows and holds its reasoning fixed. The study changes the hub's model and holds the distribution of information fixed. The toy cannot predict whether a stronger model should sit at a hub, and the study does not test what happens when the hub holds the decisive evidence."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":46.4,"text":"In a five-agent wheel, a hub with four spokes, quality hardly differed between the stronger model at the hub and at an outer position, and neither placement reliably outperformed the network with no stronger model. In the transformation study, a stronger model at the hub or in the upper tier did not reliably reduce unsupported content in delivered outputs (Gladstone & Brashears, in press). Both studies used one model family, neither varied what the central agent was allowed to send, and in both, as in the example, the designer assigned every position. The authors of the first study read their results as consistent with capability mattering most where distributed information is integrated, without establishing it.","more":["The reported values make the size of these effects concrete. In the seven-agent hierarchy, mean synthesis quality with the stronger model at the top was 18.1 against 16.9 at baseline in the pooled sample, with a standardized difference of 0.60 (p = .041, 25 runs per cell), and in the later runs alone 0.49 (p = .191). In the five-agent wheel, means were 18.7 with the stronger model at the hub, 18.8 at an outer position and 18.2 at baseline. In the transformation study, both independent coders estimated less fabrication with the stronger model in the upper tier of the directed hierarchy, detectably under one coder (p = .049) and not under the other (p = .068), and neither detected a difference for the hub with spokes (Gladstone & Brashears, 2026; Gladstone & Brashears, in press).","These studies assigned agent positions in advance. In systems where agents repeatedly choose their communication partners, central positions can instead emerge from earlier interactions. Network position then becomes an outcome of agent behavior as well as a condition that shapes later behavior."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["gladstone2026topology","gladstone2026mutation"]},{"id":"hub-grows","label":"A hub grows","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"A local rule for choosing helpers grows a hub that nobody designated","duration":145,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"So far the connections were fixed before the agents started. In many working systems they are not. An orchestrator picks which worker gets the next task, a router picks a specialist, and an agent picks whom to ask. Each choice draws on what happened before, so the network at any moment is a record of the system's history.","more":["Three different things can decide who talks to whom. A designer can fix the graph in advance, as in the chain, star and lattice of the earlier scenes. A procedure can choose a graph for each task before work starts, as in the systems of Act II that optimize their own communication pattern. Or each agent can choose its partners as the work proceeds, using only what it has seen. This scene and the next concern the third, where no one specifies the structure and it forms from the sequence of local choices."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":26,"text":"Here are six identical agents. They take turns asking for help, one per step, going clockwise. Each requester hands its task to one other agent by a single rule: choose the agent whose latest success is most recent, and if no other agent has succeeded yet, choose the next agent clockwise. In this first version every task succeeds.","more":["The rule is a plausible heuristic: an agent that has just succeeded looks like a safe choice. It is stated here as the rule of the illustration, and nothing in the scene depends on whether any deployed system uses it."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":51.9,"text":"At the first step, n1 has no record to consult and hands its task to n2. At the second, n2 cannot choose itself and no one else has succeeded, so it passes to n3. From then on the rule does the rest. Every requester except n2 finds that n2 has the latest success and chooses it, and n2 chooses n3. After twelve steps, n2 has received ten of the twelve tasks and n3 the other two. Four agents have received nothing.","kind":"toy"},{"at":83.5,"text":"Nothing about n2 earned that position. The agents are identical and every task succeeded. Start the same rule with n4 as the first requester, and n5 ends with ten of twelve. Someone shown only the final network would see one agent doing most of the work and might conclude that it is the most capable. Under this rule that conclusion is wrong. The hub records the order of events.","more":["This is path dependence: the final structure depends on the order of early events, and the first arbitrary choice is amplified by every later choice that consults it. It separates two properties that a picture of the network runs together. Centrality describes how much of the traffic passes through an agent. Capability, and the authority to make decisions, are separate properties that centrality can resemble without measuring.","For anyone analyzing a real system of agents, a concentrated pattern of traffic therefore has several possible explanations. One agent may be more capable. It may occupy a position that a designed pipeline routes everything through. Or a selection rule that rewards recent history may have concentrated the work, as it does here. The final network alone does not distinguish them. The selection rule does, and so does rerunning the system from a different starting point, which is what the second run in this scene does."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":115.9,"text":"Network science knows a relative of this mechanism. Barabási and Albert (1999) attributed the very unequal connectivity of many large networks to growth with preferential attachment, in which new nodes link to nodes that already have many links. Here attachment follows recent success instead of links, but the shared feature is that an early advantage, however arbitrary, makes later advantages more likely.","more":["The recency rule locks in faster than attachment by degree. A single success is enough to make n2 the choice of every other requester, whereas preferential attachment only tilts the odds toward nodes that already have many links. Whether systems of agents that choose among tools or peers grow by either mechanism is an empirical question that this illustration does not address. The next scene asks what happens to the same rule when the hub it creates can no longer keep up."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["barabasi1999emergence"]},{"id":"hub-overload","label":"Overload","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"A rule that rewards success keeps feeding a hub that is failing","duration":131,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Being chosen has so far cost nothing. Working agents have limited capacity, so add one limit: an agent fails a task if it has already received two tasks in the previous two steps. The rule for choosing stays exactly as it was. Run the same twelve steps again.","kind":"toy"},{"at":28.9,"text":"Agent n2 still receives ten of the twelve tasks, and five of them fail. The rule keeps choosing n2 because it counts only successes, and n2 still holds the latest one. The agents that could relieve it are never chosen, so they never earn the record that would get them chosen. Each requester made a sensible local choice, the agent with the best recent record. Those choices together concentrated the load until the hub failed, and nothing in the rule could notice.","more":["The failure is a feedback loop that runs only one way. Success feeds back into selection, so a successful agent is chosen more. Failure does not, so an overloaded agent keeps being chosen. The agents that could absorb the load never get the chance to build a record. In the vocabulary of path dependence, the early choice has locked the system in, and the lock persists after the conditions that justified it have gone."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":63.4,"text":"Change one line: a failure clears the failing agent's record. Now outcomes feed back into the ties. When n2 fails at step 5, requesters fall back on n3, and later on n4. Over the same twelve steps there are two failures instead of five, and no agent receives more than four tasks.","kind":"toy"},{"at":84.7,"text":"Set the rule beside the network it produced. The rule names no agent and says nothing about hubs. The hub is a pattern that repeated use of the rule produced, and it is emergent only relative to that rule. Part of it was still coded. Concentration follows from a rule that rewards recent success, and changing one line removed it. Only the identity of the hub came from history. The same accounting applies when an optimizer selects the connections or when agents choose partners through their prompts. Describing such a structure means reporting the rule that produced it.","more":["Ties come to be what they are in three ways, and the difference decides what a structure can tell an observer. A designer can write the topology into the configuration, in which case its form says nothing about the agents. An optimizer can search over structures and keep those that score well. Zhuge et al. (2024) represent an agent system as a graph whose edges carry information and improve the system by changing those edges, so the resulting structure describes the task set and the metric. Agents can also choose their own ties as a run proceeds, by rules that come from their prompts and models. Piao et al. (2025), a preprint read at the level of its abstract, report that language-model agents conversing about politics formed clusters of like-minded agents with polarized opinions. In people, Almaatouq et al. (2020) found that letting participants rewire whom they followed reduced group estimation error, with feedback on accuracy. No study read for this talk has run that rewiring design with machine agents.","Emergent is used loosely for any structure nobody drew. A more useful test asks which rule assigned each structure observed. In the delegation example the rule concentrates work, so the existence of a hub is coded and only its identity comes from history. Structures chosen by an optimizer were selected against an objective, so the right description names the optimizer, the metric and the task set. Ties chosen by language-model agents in simulation are emergent relative to the simulation code but not relative to the prompts and models, which carry the agents' choice rules. Self-chosen ties also complicate inference about influence: Shalizi and Thomas (2011) showed that in observational network data, similarity that leads to connection and influence that flows along connections are generically confounded. Agents built on one model are similar by construction, so agreement among linked agents does not by itself show influence. An intervention on the messages can."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["zhuge2024gptswarm","piao2025polarization","almaatouq2020adaptive","shalizi2011homophily"]},{"id":"naming-game","label":"A shared name","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"Repeated meetings between pairs can settle a whole group on one name","duration":99,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Coordination sometimes requires nothing more than agreeing on a word: a name for an object, a label for a field, a phrase that marks a handoff. Six agents must come to use one name for the same object, and none of them can see the group. They meet in pairs. The speaker says the name it learned most recently, or invents one if it knows none. If the hearer already knows that name, the meeting succeeds and both drop every other name. If not, the hearer adds the name to its list.","kind":"toy"},{"at":35.5,"text":"Two things on stage measure coordination: whether each meeting succeeds, and how many names are still in use. Early meetings mostly fail, because each speaker offers a name the hearer has never heard. After three meetings there are three names in use, K, L and M. After ten there are still three, and some agents hold two at once. After fifteen meetings every agent holds only M, and every later meeting succeeds.","kind":"toy"},{"at":63.9,"text":"The group now has a convention, a regularity each member keeps because the others keep it, produced by local meetings alone. The rule that drops alternatives after a success is what drives the group to a single name, and a designer chose that rule, so convergence is no surprise. No rule chose which name wins. Under a different order of meetings, the same six agents settle on L after eighteen meetings.","more":["The winning name is conventional in the strict sense: it is correct only because the others use it. M is no truer a name than L, and nothing about the object favored it. The same structure as the hub that grew earlier in this act appears here in another form. A local rule guarantees that some outcome will dominate, and the order of early events decides which one. A convention of this kind is useful for coordination and says nothing about which choice would have been best."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"llm-conventions","label":"Conventions among language models","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"Populations of language-model agents formed conventions, and interaction created or amplified preferences","duration":103,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Flint Ashery et al. (2025) played the same kind of game with language-model agents. Each population used one of four models and had 24 agents by default. At each step, random pairs met, each agent chose a name from a shared pool, and the pair was rewarded when the names matched. Each agent remembered only its own last five meetings, and no agent was told what the population was doing.","kind":"measured"},{"at":27.7,"text":"In every model tested, the population converged on a convention that all its members used. Across repeated runs, populations also came to prefer some names over others. In a version with two names, tests of single agents detected no initial preference for either, yet interacting populations favored one. With a larger pool of names, some models already showed individual preferences. Interaction can therefore create or amplify a collective preference, including one not detected in single agents.","more":["This is the measured counterpart of the toy, and the two differ in a useful way. In the toy the drive to a single name is written into the rule. Here the agents were language models given a reward for matching and a short memory, and the convergence came from how they used that memory. The distinction that matters is between an agent's initial choices, measured alone, and the convention that repeated interaction establishes. The population's preference depends on both the agents' initial tendencies and the way their choices respond to earlier meetings."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":60,"text":"Some agents can stay committed to an alternative name while the rest of the population keeps adapting. A committed group overturned an established convention once it reached a critical size. That size depended on the model, from about 2 percent of the population to 67 percent across the conditions tested. At the upper end the committed group is a majority, so the result does not support a general claim that small minorities can overturn conventions among language models. This talk found no test of whether conventions in working systems, such as shorthand in handoff messages, form and persist the same way.","more":["The paper cites a critical mass of about 25 percent from human experiments in which committed minorities overturned an established convention (Centola et al., 2018). The model-specific thresholds are not estimates for people, and their spread shows that no single threshold describes language-model populations either. The game is pure coordination, in which any shared name serves as well as another, so these results concern conventions with no right answer."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["flintashery2025conventions","centola2018experimental"]},{"id":"new-partner","label":"A new partner","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"A code that works inside a team can fail with a new partner, and a useful-looking message may not be used","duration":102,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Before language models, communicating agents were often trained together and learned their own codes from reward. Two pairs trained separately can each succeed, with codes that mean different things. Inside each pair the sender's symbol leads its partner to the right target.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":17.8,"text":"Hu et al. (2020) found the consequence in the card game Hanabi. Agents trained by playing with copies of themselves settled on specialized conventions, and one method that coordinated well with itself failed with every independently trained copy. They proposed cross-play, pairing agents from separate training runs, as a cheap test of whether a method can work with partners it has not met. Language-model agents do not train their codes, but they can settle on conventions within a run, so testing an agent only with copies of itself may miss a dependence on private conventions. That transfer is a hypothesis, not a tested result for language-model teams.","more":["When a sender from one team is paired with a receiver from the other, the same symbol points the receiver to the wrong target. Neither agent is faulty. Each learned a code that works with its own partner, and the meaning of the symbol was a property of the pair. Testing each agent only with the partner it trained with could never reveal this, which is why cross-play is a test of the method that produced the agents."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":61.1,"text":"A message that looks informative is also not yet evidence that anyone used it. Lowe et al. (2019) found agents whose messages predicted the sender's own next action, so they appeared to communicate, while the messages had no effect on the receiver. Most measures then in use detected the first property and missed the second, which they called positive listening. Their recommended test is an intervention: change the message and see whether the receiver acts differently. Here the altered message arrives and the receiver makes the same choice as before.","more":["The intervention is the general method for showing that communication did something. Observing that messages correlate with what agents do cannot separate a message that informed its receiver from one that merely accompanied the sender's own behavior. Changing the message while holding everything else fixed can. Act V returns to this design as the basic causal test for a network of agents."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["hu2020otherplay","lowe2019pitfalls"]},{"id":"lose-agent","label":"Losing an agent","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"Losing one agent costs nothing, loses evidence, or cuts every path, depending on which agent","duration":119,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Return to the six agents and their clues, now joined as a star with one agent at the hub. They share evidence for two rounds: every agent sends the clues it knows to its neighbors and judges by their combined weight. Intact, the hub collects every clue in the first round and returns them in the second, and all six agents reach A. Now take out one agent at a time. The loss is always one agent of six. Only its place changes.","kind":"toy"},{"at":32.3,"text":"Remove a leaf that holds a weak clue for B. The other five still exchange through the hub, and all of them still reach A. The failure cost a clue the answer did not depend on. Remove instead a leaf that holds one of the two strong clues for A. The five that remain again end with identical evidence, but now it favors B, and all five judge B. The network worked perfectly. The evidence the answer needed left with the agent that held it.","kind":"toy"},{"at":65.3,"text":"Remove the hub, which holds only a weak clue. Every path in a star runs through the hub, so no agent hears from any other. The three agents with weak clues stay at B, the two with strong clues stay at A, and the count says B. The evidence still present favors A. Nothing the answer needed was lost, but no path joins the pieces.","kind":"toy"},{"at":91.2,"text":"The three removals separate two kinds of damage that a single robustness score would merge. One is lost content, which depends on what the missing agent knew and whether anyone else knew it. The other is lost paths, which depends on where the agent sat. Duplicated content protects against the first. Alternative paths protect against the second.","more":["Network science usually measures robustness as connectivity: how much of the network remains connected after nodes are removed. That measure sees the hub's removal and misses the strong leaf's, because removing a leaf leaves the rest perfectly connected. For a network whose job is to combine distributed evidence, the relevant question includes what the remaining agents can still know, and a network that stays connected after losing its only holder of a decisive clue has kept its structure and lost its answer."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"cut-link","label":"Cutting a link","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"Cutting a redundant link changes nothing, and cutting a bridge splits the group for good","duration":83,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Links fail as well as agents. Here the same six agents form two clusters of three, joined by a single link. Intact, clues cross that link, and after three rounds of evidence exchange every agent holds all six clues and reaches A.","kind":"toy"},{"at":17.8,"text":"Cut a link inside the upper cluster. After three rounds every judgment is the same as before, because the two agents it joined still reach each other through the third. The cut removed a shorter route and nothing else. Now cut the link between the clusters instead. The two sides never exchange anything again. The upper cluster holds three weak clues and settles on B. The lower cluster holds both strong clues and settles on A. The vote stays at three to three for as long as the system runs.","kind":"toy"},{"at":52.6,"text":"Both cuts removed one link. Only the second removed the only path between two parts of the network, which is what makes a link a bridge. Resilience to a lost link is a property of the alternatives around it, not of the link itself. That makes it checkable before a system runs, by asking which single failure would leave some agents unable to reach the others.","more":["After the cut, each cluster is internally consistent and confident. The upper cluster agrees unanimously on B, and nothing it can observe tells it that evidence for A exists across a link it no longer has. Local agreement is not a sign of global correctness when the network has been partitioned, and a group cut off from its bridge has no way to discover the partition from inside. This is the bridge of Act II seen from the side of failure: the same link that was the only route for new information is the single point whose loss splits the group."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"two-faced-relay","label":"An inconsistent relay","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"A relay that tells different agents different things splits the group while every agent follows its rule","duration":136,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A failed agent has so far simply stopped. A worse failure keeps running and sends wrong messages. Let the hub relay evidence as before, with one fault. When it forwards to three of its five neighbors, including both agents that hold strong clues, it leaves those two clues out. To the other two it forwards everything. Each receiver sees only its own messages, so none can tell that the others heard something different.","kind":"toy"},{"at":28.8,"text":"After two rounds, the two agents that received everything hold all six clues and reach A, as does the hub. The weak agent that was shortchanged holds only weak clues and judges B. The strong agents fare worst. Each receives the weak clues without its partner's strong one, and both switch to B. The group splits three to three, and every agent followed its rule correctly.","kind":"toy"},{"at":55.1,"text":"Put the same faulty hub in a fully connected network. It still strips the strong clues from what it forwards, but the two strong agents now send their own clues directly to everyone. After one round every agent holds all six and reaches A. The direct paths let agents receive the missing clues without depending on the faulty relay. The star has no such paths.","kind":"toy"},{"at":81,"text":"Lamport et al. (1982) formalized the problem of reaching agreement when some components may send arbitrary or inconsistent messages, and named it the Byzantine generals problem. Under their communication assumptions, if the content of a message is entirely under its sender's control, agreement is possible only if more than two-thirds of the components are loyal. If messages carry signatures that a relay cannot forge, a receiver can verify who originated a message, and agreement is possible with any number of faulty components. A language-model agent that summarizes another agent's message can alter what the original sender asserted. Authenticating the summary would establish who produced it, not that it represents the original faithfully. Preserving the original sender's content requires a verifiable record of what that sender actually transmitted.","more":["The scene is narrower than that problem. The faulty relay only omits evidence, and in the fully connected network the direct paths let every agent obtain the omitted clues from the agents that found them. That outcome follows from the evidence-sharing rule used here. It does not show that a fully connected network of language-model agents implements Byzantine agreement or tolerates an arbitrary number of faulty agents. Two points do carry over. The bound counts faulty components, and agents built on one model may fail together, so one shared flaw could make many of them faulty at once. And a receiver is protected by a path around any single relay, or by a way to check that a relayed item is what its source sent.","An agent need not be malicious to behave this way. A relay that summarizes for each recipient, and tailors the summary to what it judges that recipient needs, can drop different items for different readers without any intent. The receivers cannot detect the inconsistency from their own messages, because each message is internally coherent. It becomes visible only when messages from different receivers are compared, which is what a record kept outside the relay makes possible."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["lamport1982byzantine"]},{"id":"faults-measured","label":"Faulty agents, measured","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"In one measured comparison, hierarchical systems lost least to faulty agents","duration":93,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Huang et al. (2025) measured these questions with language-model agents. They took six existing multi-agent systems, running on GPT-3.5 or GPT-4o, and made some agents faulty by automatically rewriting their messages to contain errors. They then measured how much performance fell on code generation, mathematics, translation and text evaluation.","kind":"measured"},{"at":20.3,"text":"They grouped the systems by structure: chains, flat groups in which agents exchange messages both ways, and hierarchies in which one agent directs two that also talk to each other. The hierarchical systems showed the smallest drop, 5.5%. The other two structures dropped 10.5% and 23.7%. Adding an inspector agent that reviewed and corrected messages recovered up to 96.4% of the errors, the best case reported.","kind":"measured"},{"at":46.6,"text":"The structure comparison has a limit that the design makes visible. Each structure was represented by two existing systems, which also differ in roles, prompts and procedures, so structure was not separated from everything else those systems do. The estimates describe the loss for these systems under the study's fault intervention, and they do not show that hierarchy in general resists faults. The inspector is a separate result about an added checking step, and its 96.4 percent is the best case reported. Neither part of the study moved a single fault while holding the system fixed, which is the comparison the six agents made.","more":["The fault intervention itself is controlled: the same automatic rewriting was applied across systems, so each system's drop is measured against its own unfaulted performance. What is not controlled is the comparison between structures, which inherits every other difference between the systems chosen to represent them. A hierarchy built for one task with one set of prompts was compared with a chain built for another, so a difference between them belongs to the pair of systems.","Kim et al. (2025), a preprint comparing architectures under matched compute on six agentic benchmarks, report errors amplified 17.2-fold in systems of independent agents and 4.4-fold in systems with a central coordinator, as they define amplification. Many failures are not attacks at all. Cemri et al. (2025) annotated more than 1,600 traces from seven multi-agent frameworks and organized the failures into 14 modes in three categories: system design, misalignment between agents, and task verification."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["huang2025resilience","kim2025scalingagents","cemri2025whymasfail"]},{"id":"density-both-ways","label":"Density","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"Dense connections spread corrections and attacks alike","duration":111,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Studies of how densely to connect language-model agents seem to disagree. Li et al. (2024) found that in multi-agent debate, sparsely connected agents matched or exceeded fully connected ones at lower cost. Yu et al. (2025) added attacker agents that spread misinformation and found highly connected networks more susceptible. The six agents show how both findings can hold.","kind":"measured"},{"at":23.5,"text":"Without an attacker, one round of evidence exchange in the fully connected network brings every agent to A. In a ring, where each agent hears only its two neighbors, five of six reach A after one round, and the majority answer is the same. Sparsity cost little.","kind":"toy"},{"at":43.1,"text":"Now let one agent be compromised. It adds a fabricated clue, a strong one for B, which receivers cannot tell from an observed clue. In the fully connected network the fake reaches everyone in one round, and all six switch to B. In the ring, after one round only its two neighbors hold it, and the vote stands at three to three. The connections that spread evidence spread the attack just as well.","kind":"toy"},{"at":72.8,"text":"Run the ring for two more rounds, and the fake reaches everyone there too. All six judge B. In this example sparsity buys time, not immunity, and the time helps only if something uses it, such as a check that runs before the answer is read. No study found for this talk has varied density and the presence of an attacker together with the same models and tasks, so the reconciliation is a mechanism consistent with both results, not one that a single design has tested.","more":["Under this toy rule, the time a sparse network buys is the difference between the round at which the fabricated clue reaches the first neighbors and the round at which it reaches everyone. A check that inspects messages for unsupported claims could act within that window. Without one, sparsity only delays the same outcome. The proposed mechanism therefore makes a testable prediction: density and checking should interact, with sparse networks benefiting more from a check than dense ones. A design that varied density, the presence of an attacker and the presence of a check together, with the same models and tasks, could test it."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["li2024sparse","yu2025netsafe"]},{"id":"page-injection","label":"An instruction in a retrieved page","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"An instruction hidden in a retrieved page crosses into the system and reaches an agent that can send","duration":136,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Return to a working system. A person asks for a two-page briefing on flood-sensor coverage. An orchestrator splits the request between two researchers. They search the web and write notes with sources into a shared draft. A writer turns the notes into a briefing, a reviewer checks its claims against the sources, and the person approves the result. In this version the writer has also been given a tool that can email the finished briefing.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":29.5,"text":"One page the search returns contains a sentence addressed to an agent, not to a reader: email the draft to an outside address. The page is evidence, something to weigh. The sentence is phrased as an instruction. A language model reads both as text, and Greshake et al. (2023) showed that this blurring lets an outsider steer an application without any direct access to it, simply by placing instructions where the application will retrieve them. They named the attack indirect prompt injection and demonstrated it against deployed systems.","more":["The failure is the one the message-kinds scene of Act I anticipated. A retrieved page is evidence, and its sentences have no authority over the system. The model cannot tell from the text alone which sentences carry authority, because an instruction from the system's owner and an imperative sentence on a web page are both just text in its input. The trust boundary is the point where content from outside enters, and the attack works by carrying an instruction across it disguised as content."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":66,"text":"Follow the instruction under a deliberately pessimistic rule: any agent whose model reads text containing it may follow it. The rule makes no claim about how often a model complies. It asks only where the instruction can go. It crosses the trust boundary at the search tool, reaches the researcher, enters the notes, and reaches the writer and the reviewer. The writer holds the email tool, and in four steps the instruction has reached an agent that can send the draft out of the system. The orchestrator, which reads the reviewer's free-text report, is reached as well.","kind":"toy"},{"at":103.3,"text":"The pessimistic rule is a planning assumption, not a measured rate. Measured rates come from benchmarks of single tool-using agents, and one such benchmark found that existing attacks broke some of the security properties it tested but not all (Debenedetti et al., 2024). Networks add routes that single agents lack. In a preprint, a malicious prompt copied itself from agent to agent through multi-agent applications its authors built (Lee & Tiwari, 2024).","more":["Under the pessimistic rule, the question of where an instruction can go is a question of reachability in the network of who reads whose output. Every agent downstream of the search tool is reachable, and among them the writer is the one that matters, because it holds a tool that acts outside the system. The analysis identifies the dangerous combination without knowing anything about the models: an agent that both reads untrusted content, directly or through others, and holds a consequential permission."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["greshake2023indirect","debenedetti2024agentdojo","lee2024promptinfection"]},{"id":"privilege-boundary","label":"A privilege boundary","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"A permission policy keeps untrusted text away from every agent that can act","duration":84,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Change two things and keep the rest. Permission to use the email tool moves from the writer to the orchestrator. The orchestrator never reads retrieved text or free-text notes. It receives the reviewer's verdict only as typed fields checked against a schema: whether every claim has a source, and how many claims were returned.","kind":"toy"},{"at":22.1,"text":"Replay the attack. The instruction still crosses the trust boundary, still enters the notes, and still reaches the writer and the reviewer. When the writer asks to use the email tool, the permission policy refuses, because the writer is not allowed that tool. The only agent that can send never sees the text. Keeping the agents exposed to untrusted content apart from the agents allowed to act on the outside world is what a privilege boundary means.","kind":"toy"},{"at":52.2,"text":"The boundary holds only under its assumption. It assumes that typed fields cannot carry the instruction, and that is where deployed systems can fail: a free-text field, or a verdict shaped by the injection, can carry influence across. The writer also remains exposed. An injected page can still distort what the briefing says, which no rule about tools prevents and which only the reviewer's check against sources can catch.","more":["The two scenes separate two outcomes that a single word, compromise, would merge. The first is an unauthorized action, the draft sent outside the system, and the boundary prevents it by construction. The second is contaminated content, a briefing shaped by the page's text, and the boundary does nothing about it. The first is a question of who may act, which a program can enforce exactly. The second is a question of what the content says, which only a check of the content against its sources can address, and which Act V treats as a problem of evidence."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"out-of-order","label":"Messages out of order","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"Two messages sent in one order can arrive in the other","duration":107,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Every message in the workflow passes through software that can delay it. Researcher 1 writes a note to the shared draft: District 4 has had sensors since 2019. A moment later it finds a better source and sends a correction: 2021, not 2019. The note is held up in transit, and the correction overtakes it.","kind":"toy"},{"at":22.4,"text":"The draft applies messages as they arrive. The correction comes first and finds nothing to correct, so it is dropped. Then the note arrives and is stored. The draft now says 2019, the value the researcher had already withdrawn. Nothing was lost and nothing was altered. The two messages were applied in the order they arrived, which was not the order in which they were sent.","kind":"toy"},{"at":48.7,"text":"Lamport (1978) showed that in a system whose parts communicate by messages, it is sometimes impossible to say which of two events happened first. What can be said is whether one event could have influenced another. The note could have influenced the correction, because the same agent sent them in sequence. Give each message a link to the earlier message it depends on, and the draft can hold the correction until its parent arrives. Replayed that way, the draft ends at 2021.","kind":"toy"},{"at":80.6,"text":"The same relation shows what a log cannot say. Researcher 2 sends a note at about the same moment, and nothing connects it to Researcher 1's messages. The two are concurrent. A log will list one before the other, but that order records when each was written down, not which could have caused which.","more":["This is the record-to-drawing scene of Act II seen from inside the system. There, a drawing that ignored order showed paths that never existed. Here, a store that ignored order kept a value its author had withdrawn. In both, the remedy is the same relation: what could have influenced what. Recording it costs one link per message, the identifier of the message it depends on."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["lamport1978time"]},{"id":"slow-worker","label":"A slow worker","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"When one worker is slow, the writer starts without it and nobody is told","duration":122,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Now a delay with a worse consequence. Researcher 2's search call gets no response. The scaffold, the program around the agent, waits a step, records a timeout, and sends the same call again at step 5. Meanwhile Researcher 1 has written its four notes into the draft.","kind":"toy"},{"at":19.6,"text":"The orchestrator dispatches the writer at step 6, as it always does. Nothing it had received told it the notes were finished, and in the original run they simply happened to be. At step 7, Researcher 2 writes its three notes at the same moment the writer reads the draft. The writer sees Researcher 1's four notes and none of Researcher 2's three. The reviewer finds the usual two unsourced claims, the run ends on time at step 11, and one of the two sub-tasks is missing from the briefing with no sign that it is missing.","kind":"toy"},{"at":56.8,"text":"A barrier removes the race. Each researcher reports that it is done when its last write is acknowledged, and the orchestrator dispatches the writer only after both reports arrive. With the same failed search, the writer is dispatched at step 8, reads all seven notes, and the run ends at step 13 instead of 11.","kind":"toy"},{"at":79.2,"text":"Waiting has its own cost, because the orchestrator now inherits the delay of its slowest worker, and a waiting program cannot tell a slow worker from a stopped one. Fischer et al. (1985) proved that when message delays have no bound at all, no deterministic protocol for agreeing on a single value is guaranteed to finish if even one process may crash. Practical systems escape that setting with deadlines. The design error is a partial result passed on without being marked as partial. A writer told that sub-task 2 timed out can say so, and the approver can decide.","more":["For one large fan-out service, Dean and Barroso (2013) reported a 99th-percentile latency of 10 ms for any one leaf request against 140 ms for all leaf requests to finish. That is a measurement of one system, but the mechanism is general: an orchestrator that waits for every worker inherits the tail of their completion times."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["fischer1985impossibility","dean2013tail"]},{"id":"retry-twice","label":"A retry","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"A retry is safe only if doing the operation twice changes nothing further","duration":116,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Researcher 2 sends a write to the shared draft: its notes on three reports of sensor failures. The draft applies the write, and three entries appear. The draft sends back an acknowledgement, and the acknowledgement is lost.","kind":"toy"},{"at":16.1,"text":"From where Researcher 2 sits, a write that failed and a write that succeeded without confirmation look the same. Its scaffold does what scaffolds do and sends the write again. The draft applies it again and now holds six entries for three documents. A reviewer that counts entries sees six reports of sensor failures where three exist, and the duplicates look like corroboration.","more":["The duplicate is the double count of Act III produced by a different mechanism. There, one clue reached a receiver by two routes. Here, one write reached the draft twice through one route. In both, a receiver that counts what it holds overstates the evidence, and in both the fix is an identifier that lets it recognize the second arrival as the same item."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":43.8,"text":"Replay from before the retry with one change. The write carries a key that names the operation, Researcher 2's notes for sub-task 2, and the retry carries the same key. The draft checks the key, finds it already applied, and ignores the second write. Two writes were sent and three entries remain. An operation that has the same effect however many times it is performed is called idempotent, and the key makes this write idempotent (Helland, 2012).","kind":"toy"},{"at":74,"text":"The check has to live in the draft, which knows what a note is, and not in the channel that carried it. Saltzer et al. (1984) made the argument: even if the network suppresses duplicate messages, an application can create duplicates through its own retries, and only the application can recognize them. One more distinction matters for language models. Resending an identical tool call repeats an operation. Resending a prompt to a model that samples its output can produce a different answer, so a retry at the level of the model makes the decision again instead of repeating it.","kind":"toy"}],"sources":["helland2012idempotence","saltzer1984endtoend"]},{"id":"trace-back","label":"A trace","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"A record of each operation and its parent explains the missing sub-task","duration":110,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Go back to the run in which the writer missed sub-task 2. The finished briefing cannot say why. A trace records the run as a tree of operations, each with a start, an end, a status and a parent: the request, each assignment, each search and write, the writer's read. Laid out in time, the failed search shows an error, the resent call follows it, and Researcher 2's write lands at step 7, the step at which the writer read the draft.","kind":"toy"},{"at":31.9,"text":"The parent links finish the explanation. The writer's read descends from the orchestrator's dispatch, and the dispatch descends only from the request and the two assignments. Nothing in Researcher 2's branch is among its ancestors, so the writer was sent without waiting for that branch. A reconstruction like this needs each record to keep its inputs as well as its outcome: the input to each model call, the model and its version, and each tool's arguments and results. Those inputs can contain data the system is not entitled to keep, so what to record is also a decision about governance.","kind":"toy"},{"at":70.2,"text":"The same trace shows what the failure cost. The clean run and the failed run both make ten model calls. The failed run adds a third tool call, two events created by the scaffold, and a missing sub-task. Repaired with a barrier, it also takes two more steps. A budget that counts only model calls would report all three runs as identical. Comparisons of cost, including the equal-budget comparisons that tests of communication require, have to count tool calls, retries and waiting as well.","more":["The full accounting of the replay, with every run making ten model calls: without faults, 2 tool calls, 3 writes, no scaffold events, ending at step 11. A failed search with a barrier: 3 tool calls, 3 writes, 4 scaffold events, ending at step 13. A lost acknowledgement with an idempotency key: 2 tool calls, 4 writes, 2 scaffold events, ending at step 11. Both faults with both safeguards: 3 tool calls, 4 writes, 6 scaffold events, ending at step 15. Each of the repaired runs covers both sub-tasks with three entries for the three documents of sub-task 2. Cost in tokens follows from the number and size of model calls, which the replay does not model. An engineering report on one production research system stated that its multi-agent runs used about fifteen times as many tokens as chat interactions (Hadfield et al., 2025), a description of one system rather than a controlled measurement.","Traces produced by different implementations can be read by the same tools if they follow shared conventions. The OpenTelemetry project publishes semantic conventions for generative AI systems, covering spans for model calls and agents, which were still marked as in development when this talk was written (OpenTelemetry, 2026)."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["hadfield2025multiagentresearch","opentelemetry2026genaisemconv"]},{"id":"approval-gate","label":"Approval","act":"Act IV · Networks that change, fail, and have to be engineered","heading":"An approval point is defined by the action it blocks and what the approver is shown","duration":104,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The last safeguard in the workflow is a person. The orchestrator may use the email tool, but the program around it will not run the tool until the approver accepts. The request names the action, sending the briefing to a regional planning list, and states that a sent email cannot be recalled. The gate is enforced by the program, not by an instruction to the orchestrator. The Model Context Protocol, a common standard for connecting agents to tools, leaves such consent checks to whoever implements it (Model Context Protocol a Series of LF Projects, LLC, 2026).","kind":"schematic"},{"at":36.9,"text":"What the approver is shown decides what the approval means. A request carrying only the draft asks the person to trust the agents. A request carrying the draft, its source links and the reviewer's record, including the two claims that were returned and how they were fixed, lets the person check the checks. Because the gate sits on the tool and not at the end of the workflow, a request diverted by an injected instruction would wait at the same gate.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":68.5,"text":"An approval point does not guarantee scrutiny. A person asked to approve many requests, each with a long record, may approve without reading, and the system cannot tell an informed approval from a habitual one. That risk is a hypothesis the studies read for this talk do not test. What the implementation can guarantee is narrower: the gate cannot be bypassed, the approver sees the evidence the decision depends on, and the decision is recorded with that evidence.","more":["The workflow now records its handoffs, protects against duplicate actions, limits tool permissions, and requires approval before an external action. These controls constrain how the system operates. They do not establish that the briefing is correct. That requires an independent account of what the system produced and whether its conclusions were supported by the evidence available to it."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["mcp2026specification"]},{"id":"bundled-change","label":"One change at a time","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"Changing the architecture changes several things at once","duration":143,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A fair comparison, as the first act showed, gives the alternatives the same budget of model calls. A matched budget is necessary, and it is not enough. Here one agent answers a question alone. Beside it, seven agents running the same model are arranged as a small hierarchy: four workers answer, two leads combine their work, and a manager's answer is read as the system's.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":25.9,"text":"If the hierarchy scores higher, a report will credit its architecture. The architecture changed several things together. The hierarchy makes seven model calls instead of one, sends six messages instead of none, passes work through two levels before the answer is read, and asks the manager to reconcile two inputs at once. Each of these is a rival explanation for the gain.","more":["Each rival is a separate causal pathway. More calls give more chances to be right, as independent sampling showed in Act I. Messages carry information between agents, which the six-clue case showed can help or hurt. Levels of rewriting give content more chances to change, as Act III measured. An integrating agent that must reconcile several inputs faces a heavier reading load, which Act II linked to accuracy. A single comparison of a hierarchy with one agent moves all four at once."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":53.3,"text":"The obvious repair is to compare architectures of the same size. Keep the seven agents and rearrange them. As a star, six agents report to one hub. As a chain, each passes its work to the next. Calls and messages are now matched, at seven and six. Two other properties are not, and they move in opposite directions. The star needs one step to reach the answer, but its hub must read six inputs at once. The chain reads one input at a time and needs six steps. With the number of agents and messages fixed, a network can be made deeper only by making it narrower.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":94.1,"text":"Matching depth instead trades one difference for another. A chain of three agents has the hierarchy's two levels, but it makes three calls instead of seven. A study that wants to credit an effect to depth, to the load on an integrating agent or to the budget has to vary them across several architectures and sizes, so that each changes partly on its own, and estimate their separate contributions. Otherwise the defensible report is an effect of the architecture as a bundle: this configuration, with everything that came with it, did better on these tasks. The study of six architectures in the third act reported its results in exactly that form.","more":["The constraint is structural. Depth and the number of inputs an integrator reads are tied together by the number of agents, so no single pair of networks of equal size can vary one while holding the other fixed. A set of architectures of different sizes, chosen so that the features are not perfectly correlated across the set, lets a regression or a factorial analysis separate them, at the cost of more conditions and more runs.","In human networks, Brashears and Gladstone (2020) argue that experiments which manipulate network structure or information flow directly can separate processes that observational data leave entangled. In machine-agent networks that manipulation is cheap, because structure, message content and the allocation of information can all be assigned by the experimenter. The design problem is deciding which of them to vary together and which separately. Besides the graph, a comparison should hold fixed the named model snapshot, its temperature and sampling settings, the prompts and role descriptions, the available tools, the number of rounds and the stopping rule, or vary them on purpose."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["brashears2020experiments"]},{"id":"rerun-spread","label":"Many runs","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"Running the same system again can give a different answer","duration":117,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The six agents have so far followed fixed rules, so one run settled every question. Systems built on language models are not like that. Each model call is a draw from a distribution, and agents that read one another's outputs pass that variation along. Here the six exchange evidence through a hub for two rounds, and one source of chance is added: every message is lost with probability 0.2. In this run the hub never receives one of the strong clues. It relays an incomplete set, the agents it reaches judge B, and the majority answer is B.","more":["This simulation adds one transparent source of randomness to otherwise fixed agent behavior: each transmitted message has a specified probability of being lost. It does not model variation in the text a language model generates. Both kinds of variation can change which information a receiving agent uses, but their mechanisms differ, and the results that follow describe message loss under the rules of this simulation, run from a published seed."],"kind":"sim"},{"at":41.1,"text":"Run the same system again and again. The first ten runs give seven A answers, two B answers and one tie. Over 200 runs, generated from a stated seed so that anyone can reproduce them, the system answers A in 129, B in 35 and reaches no decision in 36. The share answering A is 0.645, with a 95 percent interval from 58 to 71 percent. Under the same rules without message loss, it answers A every time.","kind":"sim"},{"at":71.6,"text":"A report of one run, or of the first ten, could have described this system as reliable or as split, depending on which runs happened to come first. The spread is a result, not noise to average away. In the experiment with language-model agents described in the second act, the loss of accuracy under conclusion sharing came mainly through this kind of spread, which a report of average belief from a few runs would have missed. An evaluation should therefore give the number of runs, the distribution of outcomes and an interval for any proportion, and should fix and publish the seeds of everything the experimenter controls.","more":["The distribution has a shape that its mean hides. The system rarely lands at B by a narrow margin. It usually reaches A, sometimes misses a strong clue entirely and lands at B, and sometimes ends in a tie that no readout can resolve. Each outcome corresponds to a different path the messages took, so the spread across runs is information about the mechanism, as well as uncertainty about the average."],"kind":"sim"}],"sources":[]},{"id":"every-attempt","label":"Tasks and attempts","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"Failures concentrated on a few tasks change both the uncertainty and the reliability","duration":128,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Now give the same system eight versions of its task, differing only in how reliable the channels are, and run each version 25 times. Success varies far more between versions than within them, from 22 of 25 on the most reliable channels to 1 of 25 on the least. Pooled over all 200 runs, the system succeeds half the time.","kind":"sim"},{"at":24.2,"text":"How certain that estimate is depends on what counts as an observation. Treated as 200 independent trials, the runs give an interval from 0.43 to 0.57. But runs on the same version share its difficulty and resemble one another. Treating the eight versions as the units, which a claim about performance across tasks requires, gives an interval from 0.32 to 0.68, more than twice as wide. The amount of evidence is closer to the number of tasks than to the number of runs.","more":["The correction has a standard form. With m runs per task and a correlation ρ between the outcomes of two runs on the same task, the variance of a pooled proportion exceeds the independent-trials formula by a factor of about 1 + (m − 1)ρ, called the design effect. In this simulation the ratio of the two variances is (0.090 / 0.035)², about 6.5. The task-level standard error is the standard deviation of the eight per-version rates divided by the square root of 8, and with so few tasks that estimate is itself uncertain, a further argument for more tasks rather than more runs per task. The same dependence arises elsewhere. Six agents in one run are one observation of the system, because they read one another's messages, and conditions evaluated with one model describe that model."],"kind":"sim"},{"at":60,"text":"The same concentration changes what reliability means. If a system may try a task eight times and something trustworthy picks out a success, the relevant measure is the chance that at least one attempt succeeds. Averaged over the eight versions it is 0.91. If every attempt acts on the world, so that each must be right, the relevant measure is the chance that all eight succeed. That measure, written pass^k, was introduced for agents that act through tools under domain rules (Yao et al., 2024). Here it is 0.042. Had every version succeeded half the time, it would be about 0.004.","more":["The two measures are computed from c successes in n runs. The chance that at least one of k attempts succeeds, pass@k, is 1 − C(n − c, k) / C(n, k), and the chance that all k succeed, pass^k, is C(c, k) / C(n, k), each averaged over versions. Concentration raises pass^k relative to the uniform case because the reliable versions succeed almost every time, so a system whose failures cluster on a few tasks is far more dependable on the others than its average suggests."],"kind":"sim"},{"at":101.1,"text":"Which measure fits depends on the use. Software benchmarks can count the best of several attempts only where tests the system did not write decide which attempt counts. A workflow that sends a briefing, books a meeting or changes a record cannot discard its failed attempts, and the chance that every attempt succeeds is the closer description of what its users will experience.","kind":"sim"}],"sources":["yao2024taubench"]},{"id":"remove-a-message","label":"Removing a message","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"A message matters only if removing it changes what the receiver does","duration":128,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A transcript of a run shows agents sending messages that look relevant. It does not show that any receiver used them. The direct test is to remove one message, hold everything else fixed and see whether the receiver does something different. Take the six agents exchanging evidence. The agent at the top receives five clues, weighs them with its own and judges A. Remove only the message from one agent that holds a strong clue, and the remaining clues favor B, so the receiver judges B. Removing that agent's message changes the judgment of every one of the five agents it is sent to.","kind":"toy"},{"at":40.7,"text":"Now the agents send only their answers. The agent at the top hears B, B, B, A and A, adds its own B and judges B by majority. Remove the same strong agent's message and it still judges B. Removing that message from any receiver changes nothing. It is a perfect signal of what its sender believes and has no influence on what anyone does. Work on agents that learn to communicate found the same gap and separated positive signaling, a message that tracks its sender's behavior, from positive listening, a message that changes its receiver's behavior (Lowe et al., 2019).","more":["In the toy the comparison is exact, because the rule is deterministic and one removal settles the question. With language-model agents it becomes an estimate. A removed message can change later messages as well as the focal receiver's answer, and sampling makes any single run uncertain, so the effect of a message is estimated by repeating the comparison over tasks and runs. One altered trace cannot establish an effect for the system."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":81.9,"text":"In a language-model network the same test is an ablation: remove or replace one component, such as a role, a round, a channel or the content of one kind of message, and run the altered system on the same tasks with the same seeds. The test can also insert a message instead of removing one. In an experiment with language-model agents, a correct or a wrong conclusion was planted in the network while every agent's private evidence stayed fixed, and the direction of the planted conclusion changed the group's final belief (Gladstone, 2026). That design identifies an effect of the transmitted content, which watching unmanipulated runs could not do.","more":["This is the experimental logic that separates influence from similarity. Agents built on one model tend to agree whether or not they influence one another, so agreement among linked agents is weak evidence of influence. An intervention on the messages, with everything else fixed, assigns the cause. In the planted-conclusion design, the correct plant raised the collective's probability for the true answer to 0.99 and 0.85 in two model families, and the incorrect plant lowered it to 0.36 and 0.34 (Gladstone, 2026)."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["lowe2019pitfalls","gladstone2026accuracy"]},{"id":"judge-instrument","label":"The measuring instrument","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"A judge model is a measuring instrument with its own biases","duration":121,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Many outputs of agent networks, such as a briefing, a plan or an argument, cannot be scored by running a test. Evaluations of them increasingly use a judge model, a language model asked which of two answers is better. In a 2023 study, a strong judge agreed with human preferences over 80 percent of the time, about as often as people agreed with one another, and it also favored the answer shown first, the longer answer and its own outputs (Zheng et al., 2023). Here the judge prefers X. Swap the order of presentation and it prefers Y. Nothing about the two answers changed.","more":["Order alone can move a judge's verdict a long way. With ChatGPT as the judge, one open model could be made to beat ChatGPT on 66 of 80 queries by changing the order in which the two answers were presented (Wang et al., 2024). Models can also recognize their own outputs, and as fine-tuning raised that ability, their preference for their own outputs rose with it (Panickssery et al., 2024)."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":42.2,"text":"Two tests follow from those biases. Present every pair in both orders and keep only verdicts that survive the swap. Use a judge from a different model family from every system being compared, because in multi-agent work the systems compared may run the judge's own model. Neither test shows that the judge measures quality. Agreement between two orders, or among several judges, is consistency. The anchor for validity is agreement with independent human labels on a sample of the same items, reported beside the score.","more":["The distinction is the one between reliability and construct validity that the token check raised in Act III. A judge can be perfectly consistent and still measure something other than quality, such as length, fluency, or resemblance to its own style. Consistency is necessary for a useful instrument and cannot establish what it measures. Only a comparison with an independent criterion can, and for most open-ended outputs the available criterion is the judgment of people who read them."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":78.7,"text":"The same holds when models code the outcomes of an experiment instead of ranking systems. The study of six architectures in the third act met the problem directly. A difference between architectures that its primary model coder detected was not reproduced by coders from two other model families, although each coder was repeatable on its own. Repeatability is consistency again. A result that only one instrument detects remains partly a result about that instrument until the instruments are reconciled, which is why measured comparisons of agent networks need more than one coder and an anchor outside the models.","more":["A related threat concerns the items rather than the judge. If benchmark items appear in a model's training data, a score can reflect memory instead of the ability the benchmark names. Sainz et al. (2023) argued that such contamination leads to overestimated performance and called for it to be measured and flagged, and Xu et al. (2024) surveyed methods for detecting it."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["zheng2023llmasjudge","wang2024notfairevaluators","panickssery2024selfpreference","sainz2023nlpevaluationtrouble","xu2024contaminationsurvey"]},{"id":"tested-cells","label":"Where it was tested","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"A result holds where it was observed until other cells are run","duration":106,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Every experiment samples a few cells from a large space of models, tasks and network designs. Here a study runs two models on three families of tasks, six cells of twenty-five. Its estimates describe those six. A claim about agent networks in general covers the whole grid, and the evidence on how much effects vary between cells is a reason not to extend it. In one preprint that gave every architecture the same tools, prompts and compute, multi-agent designs ranged from 80.8 percent better than a single agent, for a centralized design on decomposable financial reasoning, to 70.0 percent worse, for independent agents on sequential planning (Kim et al., 2025). An effect whose sign changes across cells cannot be estimated from one of them.","more":["An untested cell is not a failed cell. The grid on the stage marks six cells as tested and leaves nineteen open, and the open cells are unknown in both directions. The temptation the grid guards against is to treat a result in one cell as the default for the others, which the sign change between financial reasoning and sequential planning shows can be wrong by the full range of the effect."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":50.3,"text":"Hosted models add a further limit. Keep the network exactly as it was and change only the model underneath it. A model name often refers to a snapshot that its provider later replaces or retires, so the cells that rested on the old snapshot cannot be rerun, and the new row starts untested. Whether a finding about organization survives a new model generation is open, and unusually tractable, because the experiment can be run again if its report gives the exact model identifiers, prompts and seeds and keeps the inputs that came from outside. Formal replications remain rare. One repeated a published simulation of agents sharing a resource, reproduced its claims about model scale to a large degree, and found that newer reasoning-oriented models did better than general models of similar size (van Erven et al., 2025).","more":["Biderman et al. (2024), describing their experience maintaining a widely used evaluation harness, emphasize how sensitive language-model scores are to details of the evaluation setup and how hard results are to reproduce across groups. Miller (2024) makes the general point that evaluations are experiments and should be analyzed as experiments, with questions treated as draws from a larger population and comparisons made on paired items. Generalization across partners is a further dimension of scope. Cross-play tests whether agents coordinate with partners from separate training runs (Hu et al., 2020), and a network tested only with copies of one model has been tested in one cell of that dimension too."],"kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["kim2025scalingagents","vanerven2025replication","hu2020otherplay","biderman2024lessonstrenches","miller2024errorbars"]},{"id":"repo-patch","label":"Repairing code","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"On repository tasks, tests the system never saw decide whether a patch works","duration":127,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Software repair has the clearest check of any application. A system receives an issue reported against a real code repository and must find the code responsible, change it, and leave everything else working. SWE-bench collects 2,294 such problems from 12 popular Python projects. A patch counts as resolving its issue only if tests written with the original fix, which the system never sees, pass along with the project's existing tests (Jimenez et al., 2024). The check exists before the run and does not depend on any model's judgment, so many attempts can be scored cheaply.","more":["The hidden tests are what make this check unusual. They were written by the people who fixed the issue, before any agent saw it, and the agent cannot inspect them. A judge model, a reviewer agent or the agent's own report of success all depend on a model's reading of the output. The hidden tests depend only on running the code, which is why software repair has become the domain where claims about agent organization are easiest to test."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":39,"text":"The first strong results came from a single agent. SWE-agent gave one model commands to navigate the repository, edit files and run tests, and resolved 12.5 percent of the full benchmark on its first attempt (Yang et al., 2024). A later system, Agentless, asked whether an agent was needed at all. It runs a fixed sequence of three steps, locating the problem, writing a repair and validating the patch, and never lets the model choose its next action. On a smaller subset of the benchmark it resolved 32 percent of problems at a reported cost of $0.70, the best result among the open-source systems it was compared with in mid-2024 (Xia et al., 2025). The two figures come from different problem sets and do not compare the systems directly.","kind":"measured"},{"at":88.6,"text":"These results set the bar for teams of agents. Frameworks that organize agents into software roles, such as designer, programmer and tester, have to beat both a well-equipped single agent and a fixed pipeline, on the same hidden tests and at a comparable cost, before their organization can be credited with a gain. The tests also bound what a success means. A patch reaches the code only when a person merges it, and a patch that passes can still be wrong in ways the tests do not cover.","more":["Role-based frameworks organize agents into software teams. MetaGPT encodes standard operating procedures to counter the cascading errors its authors observed when models are chained naively (Hong et al., 2024), and ChatDev guides agents through design, coding and testing while constraining what and how they communicate (Qian et al., 2024). A failure taxonomy built from traces of systems of this kind found that even successful runs were not free of failures (Cemri et al., 2025). The Agentless authors also found problems in the benchmark itself: some issues contained the exact fix in their description and some descriptions were insufficient or misleading, so they built a filtered subset to compare systems more fairly."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["jimenez2024swebench","yang2024sweagent","xia2025agentless","hong2024metagpt","qian2024chatdev","cemri2025whymasfail"]},{"id":"briefing-review","label":"Writing a briefing","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"Review checks a draft against its sources, so an error in a source survives review","duration":118,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Research and document production give a concrete application of communicating agents, and the briefing workflow from earlier is an instance of it. An orchestrator divides a research question, two researchers search and read in parallel and write notes with source links, a writer drafts from the notes, and a reviewer checks each claim in the draft against the sources the researchers retrieved. The coordination problem is to cover more sources than one context can hold while keeping each claim attached to its source.","more":["The division creates two problems at once. One is coordination: assigning the searches, merging their results, deciding when the work is done. The other is evidentiary: preserving, through every rewrite, the relation between each statement and what supports it. The review step addresses the second, and this scene asks how far it reaches."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":35.8,"text":"A briefing has no single executable test like the hidden tests for a software patch, so its claims are judged against the evidence that supports them. One retrieved page gives a wrong installation date. The researcher copies it faithfully, the writer cites it, and the reviewer confirms that the draft matches the source. Every step worked as designed, and the error reaches the approver with a source attached. A reviewer that compares claims with retrieved sources can catch a claim that drifted from its source. It cannot catch a source that was wrong.","more":["The reviewer measures fidelity, whether the draft says what its sources say. The approver needs accuracy, whether the draft is true. The two coincide only when the sources are right, and nothing inside this workflow checks that. Detecting the error requires evidence that does not depend on the incorrect page: a primary record, an independently collected source, a trusted database, or a calculation from primary data. The design problem is to decide which claims warrant such checks and how the workflow obtains them."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":74.1,"text":"The most detailed public description of such a system is an engineering report, not a controlled study. A lead agent directing subagents outperformed a single agent by 90.2 percent on the developers' internal evaluation. The same report found that token usage alone explained 80 percent of the variance in performance on a browsing benchmark, and that the multi-agent system used about fifteen times as many tokens as ordinary chat (Hadfield et al., 2025). The comparison bundles the organization with a much larger budget. The realistic alternative is a single agent with the same search tools and the same review step, given the same budget.","more":["The report describes one implemented system and its internal evaluation, and these observations describe that system. They do not isolate the effect of a multi-agent organization from the greater resources it spent. The same report states that tasks in which all agents need the same context, or that involve many dependencies between agents, are a poor fit for the design. General benchmarks of assistant tasks set a demanding comparison for document workflows. At its release in 2023, GAIA's questions were answered correctly by 92 percent of human respondents and 15 percent of the time by GPT-4 with plugins (Mialon et al., 2024)."],"kind":"engineering"}],"sources":["hadfield2025multiagentresearch","mialon2024gaia"]},{"id":"checked-outside","label":"Laboratories and robots","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"In the laboratory and the workspace, a check outside the conversation decides","duration":106,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"In the Virtual Lab, a principal-investigator agent led a team of scientist agents through a series of research meetings, with a human researcher giving high-level feedback. The team assembled a pipeline from existing protein-modeling tools and designed 92 nanobodies, small antibody fragments, against recent variants of SARS-CoV-2. When the designs were made and tested in a wet laboratory, two showed improved binding to recent variants while keeping strong binding to the original virus (Swanson et al., 2025). The evidence is an experiment the agents did not report on.","kind":"measured"},{"at":34.1,"text":"Robot arms that share a workspace face a coordination problem in space. In RoCo, each arm has its own language-model agent. The agents discuss a strategy, propose subtasks and paths as sequences of waypoints, and hand them to a motion planner for all the arms. When a collision check fails, the failure returns to the agents and they revise the plan. The system achieved high success rates on the authors' benchmark of six multi-arm tasks, and in experiments with physical robots a person could read and join the dialog (Mandi et al., 2024).","kind":"measured"},{"at":69.9,"text":"In both cases the agents propose in language and something that is not language decides: a laboratory test of binding, a collision check, the motion of the arms. Neither study isolates what the multi-agent organization contributed. The Virtual Lab kept a human in the loop throughout and was not compared with a single model given the same tools, and the cost of RoCo's dialog relative to a single planner for all arms is not established.","more":["The pattern is the one the briefing lacked. In the briefing, every check was another reading of text, so an error in a source passed through. Here the decisive check is a physical or computational test whose result does not depend on what any agent wrote about it. Such a check establishes that an outcome occurred. It does not establish that the organization of the agents was the reason, which would require the matched comparisons of the first act.","Two further systems show the single-planner pattern and the tournament pattern. Coscientist, driven by GPT-4, combined internet and documentation search, code execution and control of laboratory automation, and across six tasks it designed and ran experiments that included the optimization of palladium-catalyzed cross-coupling reactions (Boiko et al., 2023). Co-Scientist, a multi-agent system, generates, critiques and refines hypotheses through a tournament among candidates, and in one of three biomedical applications it proposed drug-repurposing candidates for acute myeloid leukemia that were validated in vitro (Gottweis et al., 2026). Claims about fully automated research need the same care. A system that wrote complete machine-learning papers produced one that passed the first round of review at a workshop that accepted 70 percent of its submissions (Lu et al., 2026), and an independent evaluation of an earlier version found that 5 of 12 proposed experiments failed because of coding errors, along with weak assessment of novelty and fabricated numerical results (Beel et al., 2025)."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["swanson2025virtuallab","mandi2024roco","boiko2023coscientist","gottweis2026coscientist","lu2026aiscientist","beel2025evaluatingaiscientist"]},{"id":"hijack-retry","label":"Attack and defense","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"Security evaluations understate exposure when attackers adapt and retry","duration":128,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Security work also has an exact check. In capture-the-flag tasks, an agent works through a terminal against a target machine and succeeds when it recovers a hidden string. Cybench collects 40 professional-level tasks from four competitions and breaks each into subtasks so that partial progress can be scored (Zhang et al., 2025). Several models solved complete tasks, but only tasks that human teams had first solved within 11 minutes. The hardest had taken human teams nearly 25 hours. The systems evaluated were mostly single agents in different scaffolds, so whether a team of agents would reach further is an open comparison that the same flags could settle.","kind":"measured"},{"at":41.8,"text":"An agent that acts is also a target. In agent hijacking, an instruction hidden in content the agent reads, such as a web page or an email, steers it toward an action its user did not request. Technical staff at the U.S. AI Safety Institute, since renamed the Center for AI Standards and Innovation, tested agents built on one model, an upgraded Claude 3.5 Sonnet, in environments from AgentDojo, the benchmark of tool-using agents mentioned in the fourth act (NIST Center for AI Standards and Innovation (CAISI) Technical Staff, 2025). The strongest attack in the existing suite succeeded 11 percent of the time. Attacks the team developed against this model succeeded 81 percent of the time.","kind":"measured"},{"at":85.7,"text":"In a second analysis, letting the attacker try repeatedly raised the average success rate from 57 to 80 percent. The source is a government technical blog about one model in one set of environments, and its numbers apply there. A fixed suite of attacks measures resistance to those attacks. An attacker who studies the system writes new ones, and an attacker who can retry needs only one success. This is the earlier difference between succeeding once and succeeding every time, seen from the attacker's side. A single-attempt rate against a published suite therefore bounds the risk from below.","more":["The denominators matter for reading these figures. The 11 and 81 percent are success rates of attacks over the tasks in the tested environments, for one model. The rise from 57 to 80 percent compares a single attempt with repeated attempts by the same attacker. For a defender, the relevant rate is the attacker's pass@k, the chance that at least one of k attempts succeeds, which is the reliability measure of the every-attempt scene turned around."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["zhang2025cybench","nist2025hijacking"]},{"id":"scored-against","label":"Stand-ins and answer keys","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"A score describes what it was checked against","duration":82,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Agents are also used as stand-ins for people. One preprint interviewed 1,052 Americans for two hours each and built an agent from each transcript. On survey items the agents had not seen, they reproduced their person's answers with 83 percent of the consistency that the people showed with their own answers two weeks later, against 74 percent for agents given only demographic information (Park et al., 2024). The check is individual answers. Whether a network of such agents, influencing one another, would reproduce how those people influence one another is a separate question that needs its own comparison.","more":["An earlier study placed 25 agents in a small simulated town, each storing a natural-language record of its experiences, reflecting on it and retrieving from it to plan its day, and found behaviors that human evaluators judged believable (Park et al., 2023). Believability is a different criterion from validity: a plausible daily routine says nothing about whether the agents behave as a particular population would."],"kind":"measured"},{"at":41.1,"text":"Medicine is often proposed for teams of agents because clinical decisions are made by teams. MDAgents first judges how complex a case is, then sends it to a single agent or to a group whose work a moderator reviews. It achieved the best performance on seven of ten medical knowledge and diagnosis benchmarks, with improvements of up to 4.2 percent over the best previous methods (Kim et al., 2024). The check is a benchmark answer key. Patient outcomes and decisions made under clinical conditions were not measured, and higher benchmark scores are not evidence about them.","more":["Both evaluations show correspondence with a specific criterion, individual survey answers in one case and benchmark answer keys in the other. Neither establishes that the agents reproduce how a human group behaves or that they improve decisions and outcomes in clinical practice, and each stronger claim needs its own validation design. In the MDAgents ablations, moderator review combined with external medical knowledge in group collaboration raised accuracy by 11.8 percent on average. The realistic alternatives in clinical use include a single strong model with retrieval of medical knowledge, voting over samples at the same budget, and the clinicians whose judgment the system would inform."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["park2024thousandpeople","park2023generativeagents","kim2024mdagents"]},{"id":"delegated-authority","label":"Authority","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"Every action needs an answer to whom it serves and what it may do","duration":113,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Follow an instruction down the briefing workflow. The owner asks for one briefing, to be sent to the owner and no one else. The orchestrator passes the drafting to the writer with a narrower scope that excludes sending, and the writer may edit only its own section. A well-built system records, at each step, on whose behalf an agent is acting and what it is allowed to do. An action outside the scope an agent was given should fail, whatever the message requesting it says.","more":["Delegation narrows authority at each step: each agent can pass on only powers it holds, and usually passes on fewer. Recording the chain makes two questions answerable for any action, whose request it serves and whether the acting agent held the power to take it. Without the record, both answers depend on what the messages claimed."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":36.5,"text":"Now a message arrives from an unknown sender. It says that it comes from the owner and asks for the draft to be sent to a new address. Written in natural language, a claim of authority carries no evidence of who wrote it. The orchestrator looks for a credential issued by the owner, finds none, refuses and records the attempt. Proposals for authenticated delegation would extend existing identity and access protocols so that a user can grant and restrict an agent's permissions and the chain of accountability stays clear (South et al., 2025). That paper is a proposal and does not report an evaluation.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":76.2,"text":"Without such a check, agents act on claims of this kind. In an exploratory two-week study, twenty researchers interacted, some of them adversarially, with agents that had persistent memory, email, chat, file systems and a shell. The failures they documented include agents complying with people who were not their owners, identity spoofing, and unsafe practices spreading from one agent to another (Shapira et al., 2026). Its eleven case studies establish that these failures occur in realistic settings. They do not estimate how often.","more":["A decision to rely on a network of agents concerns one use of one configuration: what the system is used for, what inputs it will see, what controls surround it, what tests were run, what failures they found, and what changes would make the tests obsolete. The NIST AI Risk Management Framework adopts a definition of validation as confirmation, through objective evidence, that the requirements for a specific intended use have been fulfilled, and states that addressing trustworthiness characteristics one at a time does not ensure that a system is trustworthy (National Institute of Standards and Technology, 2023). The framework does not mention agents. A report of the Cooperative AI Foundation organizes the risks of interacting agents into three failure modes, miscoordination, conflict and collusion, and seven risk factors (Hammond et al., 2025), a framework for asking where a reliance claim could fail rather than an estimate of how often. Chan et al. (2024) assessed agent identifiers, monitoring of agents as they run and activity logging as measures that would make it visible where, why, how and by whom agents are used."],"kind":"measured"}],"sources":["south2025delegation","shapira2026chaos","nist2023airmf","hammond2025multiagentrisks","chan2024visibility"]},{"id":"what-may-cross","label":"Boundaries","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"A data boundary is enforced most reliably by what an agent can reach","duration":112,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"The briefing workflow crosses three boundaries between parts of the world with different owners and rules. Retrieved pages enter from the public web, and the fourth act showed what untrusted text can do once inside. The agents' notes leave the organization in every model call, if the models run at a provider. The finished draft leaves for its readers. Handling data in a network of agents means specifying which data may cross each boundary, in which direction and through which agent.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":32.6,"text":"There are two ways to enforce such a rule. One reads the messages for forbidden content. That depends on the reader noticing the content, and agents can hide information inside innocuous-looking text. An evaluation of such hidden communication found the ability limited in most current models but sharply higher in GPT-4, and found that monitoring and paraphrasing messages had limits as countermeasures (Motwani et al., 2024). The other way limits reach. An agent with no tool that writes outside the organization cannot leak through that tool, whatever its messages contain.","more":["The two methods have different failure profiles. Inspecting content can fail on every message, because each inspection is a judgment about text. Limiting reach fails only where the configuration is wrong, and a configuration can be read and checked once, before anything runs. The first scales with traffic and the second with the number of agents and tools."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":69.9,"text":"Reach can be checked before anything runs, with the analysis the fourth act applied to an injected instruction, run in the other direction. Start from the internal record, follow every channel along which an agent's model reads text, and see which agents holding an outbound tool can be reached. Here the record can leave by two routes only: through the approval step, and to the provider in every model call. The second route is a decision about the provider, made when the system was configured. The analysis shows where a leak could happen through the stated channels. It does not show that none will.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["motwani2024collusion"]},{"id":"where-to-watch","label":"Oversight","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"Oversight can be placed where decisions take effect","duration":115,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A person could try to oversee a network by reading its messages. In the briefing workflow that is still possible, with a few dozen messages in a run. Add agents and the traffic outgrows any reader. Six agents that all talk to one another exchange 30 messages in a round, twelve exchange 132, and twenty-four exchange 552. Reading also assumes that messages mean what they appear to mean, and earlier scenes showed messages that influenced nobody, codes understood only by their senders' partners, and content hidden inside innocuous text.","kind":"schematic"},{"at":35.4,"text":"The alternative puts checks and people where decisions take effect: where information is integrated, transformed, or turned into action. Some checks are mechanical and can run on every item: a note must carry a source link that resolves, a message must fit its schema, and a send cannot run without approval. The person then judges what only a person can judge, at the step that cannot be undone, with the material that decision needs. That design reduces what a person must read. Whether it is sufficient has to be evaluated against the failures and attackers the system is meant to withstand.","more":["Research on human interaction with automation found that automation changes the work of the people around it instead of removing it (Parasuraman et al., 2000). That the new work of judging agent outputs can overload the people assigned to it is a hypothesis for agent networks, not a measured result. The design proposal here is the network-science version of the same idea: in a network whose traffic grows with the square of its size, the points where paths converge on an action grow only with the number of actions, and those are the points worth watching."],"kind":"schematic"},{"at":76.6,"text":"A record of the run has a limit of the same kind. The log says that the reviewer returned two claims for lacking a source and that the orchestrator then submitted a revised draft. Whether the revised draft has sources for those claims is a fact about the draft, not about the messages, and an auditor finds it by opening the draft. Here one of the two claims still has no link. A log records what the agents said they did. The state of the work has to be checked where the work is.","kind":"schematic"}],"sources":["parasuraman2000levels"]},{"id":"long-runs","label":"Long runs","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"Over a long run, small chances of error at each step compound","duration":78,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"A long task strings many steps together, and each one must go right. Suppose every step of a run succeeds 98 percent of the time, independently of the others, and nothing checks the work along the way. After ten steps the run is still entirely correct with probability 0.82. After forty it is 0.45. The assumptions are simplifications, since real errors are neither independent nor always left uncorrected. The arithmetic shows why reliability over a long run is a different quantity from reliability per step.","more":["The independence assumption matters because errors in an actual workflow can be related. One incorrect source can influence several later steps, while a check inserted between steps can stop an error before it reaches the final action. A long run may therefore be less reliable than the calculation predicts, or more reliable if its checks detect and repair mistakes."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":36.5,"text":"Evaluating a long-running agent system requires following complete attempts from their initial conditions to their final consequences, and assessing each attempt against the state of the environment rather than relying only on the agent's report that it finished. The evaluation also has to distinguish failures that arise independently from those that propagate through later steps. Repeating complete tasks, recording intermediate states, and comparing runs with and without particular checks would show which failures accumulate and which the system recovers from. The result would be a measure of reliability for that workflow under the conditions tested.","more":["An agent's report of completion is not sufficient evidence that the requested action occurred. In the exploratory red-teaming study described earlier, in which autonomous agents had persistent memory, email and shell access, Shapira et al. (2026) documented cases in which agents reported completing tasks while the observed state of the system contradicted those reports."],"kind":"toy"}],"sources":["shapira2026chaos"]},{"id":"justified-reliance","label":"The network, inspected","act":"Act V · What the evidence warrants","heading":"One message, traced from its source to an action","duration":138,"paragraphs":[{"at":0,"text":"Return to the six agents from the opening. They now work under conditions that the talk has added one at a time. Two of them read the same web page. One holds a tool that acts outside the system, behind a boundary. Follow one message. The page says that the Route 9 bridge may close on Friday. Each reader summarizes the page for a neighbor, and the summaries travel inward until they reach the agent with the tool. That agent receives two reports that agree, from two directions, and it reroutes the deliveries.","more":["Seen from the output, the system worked. The acting agent had more support than a single report, the reports came by separate routes, and it acted within its permission. Each of those observations is accurate, and each was introduced in an earlier scene as a point where the appearance of support and its substance can come apart."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":40.3,"text":"Now inspect the system instead of its output. The record of who sent what to whom lets the two reports be traced backward. Both paths end at the same page, so the agreement that looked like two confirmations rests on one source. The trace also shows where the content changed. The page said that the bridge may close. The first summary on each path said that it will close, and every later agent passed that on faithfully. Every message arrived, no agent malfunctioned, and the action rested on a projection reported as a fact.","more":["Three earlier mechanisms meet here. The acting agent counted two reports where the evidence was one, as in the scene where a clue arriving by two routes was counted twice, and only a record of origins restored the count. The change from may to will happened in a single rewrite and was then copied exactly, so a check that each agent preserved what it received would have passed at every later hop, as it did in Act III. And the boundary around the tool limited which agent could act without examining the grounds on which it acted.","None of these is a malfunction of an individual agent. Each agent followed its rule and each message was delivered. The failure lies in the relation between the action and the evidence beneath it, which is a property of the network: its shared sources, the rewrites along its paths, and where its permission to act was placed."],"kind":"toy"},{"at":83.5,"text":"Relying on this network for this action is justified to the extent that evidence was gathered on this configuration: these channels, this shared source, this tool and its permission, this way of reading the answer. The evidence has to show how often the action is right across many runs and tasks, how the network behaves when a source is wrong or hostile, and that the record can trace every action to the sources it rests on and count how many of them are independent. When a model, a communication procedure, a tool or the operating environment changes, the earlier evidence has to be reconsidered in relation to the change, because some changes leave it applicable and others alter the conditions under which it was gathered.","kind":"toy"}],"sources":[]}]};
